OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026", "title": "Invariant-projection counterexamples for every irrational rotation", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "293", "family_title": "Invariant projections, hyperinvariant subspaces, and transitive algebras", "abstract_html": "For every irrational angle, we construct a continuous nonnegative circle weight with exactly one zero and logarithmic integral \\(-\\infty\\) whose weighted rotation has no nontrivial invariant projection in the associated hyperfinite type II<sub>1</sub> factor. This answers negatively the question of Zhu, Fang, and Shi, with the angle prescribed in advance. The resulting operator is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Prescribed-angle counterexample — original Theorem 1.1.** Let \\(\\theta\\in(0,1)\\) be irrational.", "statement": "<p>**Prescribed-angle counterexample — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>Let \\(\\theta\\in(0,1)\\) be irrational. Let \\(R_\\theta\\) be the tracial von Neumann algebra generated by unitaries \\(U,V\\) satisfying\n\\[\nVU=e^{2\\pi i\\theta}UV,\\qquad\n\\tau(U^mV^n)=0\\quad((m,n)\\ne(0,0)),\\qquad \\tau(1)=1.\n\\]\nThis is the hyperfinite factor of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\). Let \\(m\\) be normalized Haar measure on \\(\\mathbb T\\).</p><p>There exists a continuous function \\(f:\\mathbb T\\to[0,1]\\) such that\n\\[\nf^{-1}(\\{0\\})=\\{1\\},\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathbb T}\\log f\\,dm=-\\infty,\n\\]\nand every projection \\(p\\in R_\\theta\\) satisfying\n\\[\n(1-p)Uf(V)p=0\n\\]\nis either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). Invariance here does not require \\(p\\) to commute with \\(Uf(V)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the irrational angle before making any choices. Proposition \\(4\\) constructs its parameters. Propositions \\(8\\), \\(10\\), and \\(12\\) construct the measurable weighted rotation \\(T_*\\) and exclude every invariant projection of trace strictly between zero and one.</p><p>Proposition \\(14\\) constructs the continuous one-zero weight \\(f\\) and the affiliated injective positive operator \\(H_b\\) satisfying\n\\[\nH_bT_f=e^{-1}T_*H_b\n\\]\non an invariant domain.</p><p>Apply Proposition \\(13\\) with\n\\[\nA=T_f,\\qquad B=e^{-1}T_*,\\qquad H=H_b.\n\\]\nAny invariant projection for \\(T_f\\) would produce an invariant projection for \\(e^{-1}T_*\\) of the same trace. Multiplication by the nonzero scalar \\(e^{-1}\\) does not change invariant ranges. Proposition \\(12\\) excludes every trace strictly between zero and one.</p><p>Faithfulness of the trace therefore makes every invariant projection \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). Proposition \\(14\\) supplies continuity, the unique zero, and the logarithmic integral. Proposition \\(6\\) identifies the containing algebra with \\(R_\\theta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Hyperinvariant-subspace consequence — original Corollary 1.4.** Every separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space carries a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator with no nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace.", "statement": "<p>**Hyperinvariant-subspace consequence — original Corollary 1.4.**</p><p>Every separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space carries a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator with no nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace.</p><p>For such an operator \\(T\\), its commutant\n\\[\n\\{T\\}'=\\{A\\in B(H):AT=TA\\}\n\\]\nis a proper strongly closed transitive unital complex algebra. A subspace is hyperinvariant for \\(T\\) if every member of \\(\\{T\\}'\\) leaves it invariant; an algebra is transitive if its only common closed invariant subspaces are \\(0\\) and \\(H\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The tracial representation of \\(R_\\theta\\) is separable and infinite-dimensional. Take the operator of Theorem \\(1\\), with the norm estimate established in Proposition \\(14\\).</p><p>By Proposition \\(1\\), the projection onto any hyperinvariant subspace belongs to \\(R_\\theta\\) and is invariant for the operator. Theorem \\(1\\) therefore excludes every nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace.</p><p>A nonzero quasinilpotent operator is nonscalar. Proposition \\(1\\) makes its commutant proper, strongly closed, unital, and transitive. Unitary equivalence transfers these properties to every separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space.</p><p>As noted in original Remark 1.5, this also contradicts only the hyperinvariant-subspace assertion of Chō, Ko, and Lee, Corollary 2.8, at \\(\\mu=1\\): their stated hypotheses apply to \\(I+T\\) for the constructed nonzero quasinilpotent \\(T\\) and any conjugation \\(C\\), whereas\n\\[\n\\{I+T\\}'=\\{T\\}'.\n\\]\nThe conclusion here does not exclude ordinary invariant subspaces whose projections lie outside the containing factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Spectral consequence — original Corollary 6.2.** For every irrational \\(\\theta\\in(0,1)\\), the continuous weight constructed in Theorem \\(1\\) can be chosen so that …", "statement": "<p>**Spectral consequence — original Corollary 6.2.**</p><p>For every irrational \\(\\theta\\in(0,1)\\), the continuous weight constructed in Theorem \\(1\\) can be chosen so that\n\\[\nT_f=Uf(V)\\ne0,\\qquad\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|T_f^n\\|^{1/n}=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(T_f\\) is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent.</p>", "proof": "<p>The weight used in Theorem \\(1\\) is the weight constructed in Proposition \\(14\\). Its truncated-logarithm argument proves, for every \\(a&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\|T_f^n\\|\\le e^{-an}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(n\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|T_f^n\\|^{1/n}=0.\n\\]\nThe same proposition proves \\(T_f\\ne0\\), since \\(f&gt;0\\) almost everywhere.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Product-rotation realization — original Theorem A.1.** Let \\( X=\\mathbb T^{\\mathbb N},\\qquad \\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z, \\) with product Haar probability measure \\(\\mu\\).", "statement": "<p>**Product-rotation realization — original Theorem A.1.**</p><p>Let\n\\[\nX=\\mathbb T^{\\mathbb N},\\qquad \\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\nwith product Haar probability measure \\(\\mu\\). Coordinates are represented in \\([0,1)\\).</p><p>There exist angles \\(0&lt;\\theta_i&lt;1\\) and costs \\(c_i&gt;0\\), with\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\theta_i&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nand \\(1,\\theta_1,\\theta_2,\\ldots\\) rationally independent, such that\n\\[\nf(x)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\sum_{i\\ge1}c_i\\lfloor x_i+\\theta_i\\rfloor\\right)\n\\]\nis positive almost everywhere and\n\\[\n\\int_X\\log f\\,d\\mu=-\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(\\sigma x=x+\\theta\\). On\n\\[\nH=L^2(X,\\mu;\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)),\n\\]\nlet \\(Se_k=e_{k+1}\\), and define\n\\[\n(U\\zeta)(x)=S\\zeta(x),\\qquad\n(\\pi(a)\\zeta)(x)_k=a(\\sigma^kx)\\zeta(x)_k.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nM=W^*(U,\\pi(L^\\infty(X)))\n\\]\nis a type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual. The operator\n\\[\nT=U\\pi(f)\n\\]\nis nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent, has \\(\\Delta(T)=0\\), and has no projection \\(p\\in M\\) satisfying\n\\[\n0&lt;\\tau(p)&lt;1,\\qquad (1-p)Tp=0.\n\\]</p><p>Moreover, \\(|T|\\) has pure-point spectral type. For every finite \\(F\\subset\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\nW^*(U^n|T|U^{-n}:n\\in F)\n\\]\nis atomic, whereas\n\\[\nW^*(U^n|T|U^{-n}:n\\in\\mathbb Z)\n\\]\nis diffuse. Pure-point spectral type does not assert that every point of the spectrum is an eigenvalue.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(15\\) constructs the scales and random angles. Proposition \\(16\\) supplies a probability-one ordered orbit-sampling property simultaneously for every finite coordinate set. Fix one realization satisfying rational independence and both propositions.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nc_i=C_j\\qquad(i\\in J_j).\n\\]\nProposition \\(17\\) constructs the type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual and proves that its weighted rotation is nonzero, norm-quasinilpotent, has determinant zero, and has logarithmic integral \\(-\\infty\\).</p><p>Propositions \\(18\\) and \\(19\\) transfer invariant-projection identities from positive orbit pairs to the biased product couplings. Proposition \\(20\\) applies the conditional convolution obstruction and excludes every nontrivial invariant projection.</p><p>Finally, Proposition \\(21\\) proves the pure-point spectral type of \\(|T|\\), atomicity for every finite family of polar conjugates, and diffuseness for the full orbit algebra. These are all the assertions of the product realization.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Commutants and affiliated hyperinvariant subspaces.** Let \\(H\\) be a complex Hilbert space.", "statement": "<p>**Commutants and affiliated hyperinvariant subspaces.**</p><p>Let \\(H\\) be a complex Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(T\\in B(H)\\) is nonscalar, then \\(\\{T\\}'\\) is a proper strongly closed unital complex algebra. It is transitive exactly when \\(T\\) has no nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(M\\subset B(H)\\) is a von Neumann algebra and \\(T\\in M\\), then the orthogonal projection onto any hyperinvariant subspace for \\(T\\) belongs to \\(M\\) and is invariant for \\(T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The two assertions are Lemmas \\(1.1\\) and \\(1.2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 1.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(T\\in B(H)\\) is nonscalar, then \\(\\{T\\}'\\) is a proper strongly closed unital complex algebra.", "statement": "<p>If \\(T\\in B(H)\\) is nonscalar, then \\(\\{T\\}'\\) is a proper strongly closed unital complex algebra. It is transitive exactly when \\(T\\) has no nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace.</p>", "proof": "<p>The commutation equation is preserved by addition, multiplication, and scalar multiplication, and the identity commutes with \\(T\\). If \\(A_\\lambda\\to A\\) strongly and \\(A_\\lambda T=TA_\\lambda\\), then for every \\(\\zeta\\in H\\) both sides converge, giving\n\\[\nAT\\zeta=TA\\zeta.\n\\]\nThus the algebra is strongly closed.</p><p>If every bounded operator commuted with \\(T\\), then every rank-one orthogonal projection would commute with \\(T\\), so every nonzero vector would be an eigenvector. Applying this to \\(x,y,x+y\\) for linearly independent vectors shows that the eigenvalues are equal; hence \\(T\\) would be scalar. Properness follows.</p><p>The last assertion is precisely the two definitions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 1.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(M\\subset B(H)\\) be a von Neumann algebra and \\(T\\in M\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(M\\subset B(H)\\) be a von Neumann algebra and \\(T\\in M\\). The orthogonal projection onto any hyperinvariant subspace for \\(T\\) belongs to \\(M\\) and is invariant for \\(T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every unitary \\(W\\in M'\\) and its inverse commute with \\(T\\). Hyperinvariance therefore makes the subspace reducing for \\(W\\). Its orthogonal projection commutes with all such unitaries and hence belongs to\n\\[\nM''=M.\n\\]\nSince \\(T\\in\\{T\\}'\\), its range is invariant under \\(T\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Scalar sampling estimates — original Lemma 2.1.** Let \\(m\\ge200\\) be an integer, \\( h=m^{-3/5},\\qquad \\Delta(z)=\\min(\\{z\\},1-\\{z\\}), \\) where \\(\\{z\\}\\in[0,1)\\) denotes fractional part.", "statement": "<p>**Scalar sampling estimates — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><p>Let \\(m\\ge200\\) be an integer,\n\\[\nh=m^{-3/5},\\qquad\n\\Delta(z)=\\min(\\{z\\},1-\\{z\\}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\{z\\}\\in[0,1)\\) denotes fractional part. Define\n\\[\nw_m(z,v)=\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\Delta(z)\\ge m^{-2}\\}}\n\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\{0&lt;v&lt;h\\Delta(z)\\}}}{h\\Delta(z)},\n\\]\nwith value zero when the cutoff fails, and\n\\[\nw_m^{(1)}(z,v)=w_m(z,v),\\qquad\nw_m^{(2)}(z,v)=w_m(z-v,v).\n\\]\nFor either \\(u=w_m^{(r)}\\), put\n\\[\n\\bar u(s)=\\int_0^1u(s,v)\\,dv.\n\\]</p><p>Uniformly in \\(s\\), the supremum plus total variation of \\(v\\mapsto u(s,v)\\) is \\(O(m^{13/5})\\), hence \\(O(m^{10})\\), and\n\\[\n\\|\\bar u\\|_\\infty\\le2,\\qquad\n\\int_0^1\\bar u=1-2m^{-2},\n\\]\n\\[\n(1-2m^{-2})^2\n\\le\\int_0^1\\bar u^2\n\\le1+\\frac{2h^2}{(1-h)^2},\n\\qquad\n\\int_{[0,1]^2}u^2=\\frac2h\\log\\frac{m^2}{2}.\n\\]</p><p>For the sampling assertion, let\n\\[\n0&lt;N_0\\le N\\le m^2,\\qquad\nQ=m^{(7/10)m},\\qquad H=m^{300},\n\\]\nand let \\(\\lambda\\) be uniform on \\([1/N_0,2/N_0]\\). If \\(n\\in[Q/2,Q]\\cap\\mathbb N\\), the density \\(p_n\\) of \\(\\{n\\lambda\\}\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|p_n-1|\\le N_0/n\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p><p>If distinct such \\(n,n'\\) satisfy\n\\[\n|vn+v'n'|&gt;Nm^{1000}\n\\]\nfor every integer pair with\n\\[\n0&lt;|v|+|v'|,\\qquad |v|,|v'|\\le H,\n\\]\nthen for intervals \\(I,J\\subset[0,1)\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\mathbb P(\\{n\\lambda\\}\\in I,\\{n'\\lambda\\}\\in J)-|I||J|\n\\right|=O(m^{-80}).\n\\]\nConsequently, for complex bounded-variation functions \\(f,g\\), with\n\\[\nB(f)=\\|f\\|_\\infty+\\operatorname{Var}(f),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E[f(\\{n\\lambda\\})g(\\{n'\\lambda\\})]\n-\\left(\\int_0^1f\\right)\\left(\\int_0^1g\\right)\n\\right|\n\\le Cm^{-80}B(f)B(g).\n\\]\nAll constants are absolute and the estimates are uniform in the stated parameters for sufficiently large \\(m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first orientation, the function of \\(v\\) is constant on an interval and has height at most \\(m^{13/5}\\).</p><p>For the second orientation, nonzero values require \\(v&lt;s\\): if \\(v&gt;s\\), then\n\\[\n\\Delta(s-v)\\le v-s&lt;v,\n\\]\nwhereas \\(h&lt;1\\). On \\(0&lt;v&lt;s\\), the conditions\n\\[\n\\Delta(s-v)\\ge m^{-2},\\qquad v&lt;h\\Delta(s-v)\n\\]\ndescribe an interval. The reciprocal of \\(\\Delta(s-v)\\) has at most two monotone pieces there, with the same height bound. This proves the variation estimates.</p><p>In the first orientation, \\(\\bar u\\) is the cutoff indicator and\n\\[\n\\iint u^2\n=\\frac2h\\int_{m^{-2}}^{1/2}\\frac{ds}{s}\n=\\frac2h\\log(m^2/2).\n\\]\nThe change \\((s,v)\\mapsto(s-v,v)\\) preserves double integrals, giving the same mass and square integral in the second orientation.</p><p>To estimate the second averaged density, omit the cutoff. The resulting density \\(d\\) is the law of\n\\[\nz+t\\Delta(z)\n\\]\nfor independent uniform \\(z\\in[0,1)\\) and \\(t\\in(0,h)\\). For fixed \\(t\\), this is an increasing piecewise-linear bijection with slopes \\(1+t\\) and \\(1-t\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\int d=1,\\qquad\n|d-1|\\le\\frac h{1-h},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\int d^2=1+\\int(d-1)^2\n\\le1+\\frac{h^2}{(1-h)^2}.\n\\]\nRestoring the cutoff decreases this nonnegative density; also \\(d\\le(1-h)^{-1}\\le2\\). Cauchy–Schwarz gives the lower square-integral bound from the exact mass.</p><p>Periodizing a uniform interval of length \\(n/N_0\\) proves\n\\[\n|p_n-1|\\le N_0/n.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(p_n\\le2\\) for large \\(m\\). For every permitted nonzero frequency pair, direct integration gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E e^{2\\pi i(vn+v'n')\\lambda}\\right|\n\\le\\frac{N_0}{\\pi|vn+v'n'|}\n\\le\\pi^{-1}m^{-1000}.\n\\]</p><p>Convolve each interval indicator with the Fejér kernel\n\\[\nF_H(s)=\\frac1H\\left(\\frac{\\sin(\\pi Hs)}{\\sin(\\pi s)}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nThese convolutions lie in \\([0,1]\\), preserve means, and have Fourier coefficients of modulus at most one and frequencies below \\(H\\). Outside endpoint strips of width \\(\\eta=m^{-80}\\), the error is \\(O((H\\eta)^{-1})\\). The marginal density bounds show that replacement in either product costs\n\\[\nO(\\eta+(H\\eta)^{-1}),\n\\]\nboth under the actual law and under product Haar measure. The remaining Fourier error is \\(O(H^2m^{-1000})\\). This proves the rectangle estimate without asserting a joint density or independence.</p><p>Finally, up to a null set, a bounded-variation function is a constant plus an integral of indicators \\(\\mathbf1_{[a,1)}\\) against its complex Stieltjes measure. The constant and measure have total size at most \\(B(f)\\). Apply the rectangle estimate to the resulting products and integrate against total variation. Changes at discontinuities do not matter because the marginals have densities.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Nonzero finite convolution — original Lemma 2.2.** For every integer \\(K\\ge0\\) and real numbers \\(D\\ge\\delta>0\\), there exists \\( \\varepsilon(K,D,\\delta)>0 \\) such that complex Laurent polynomials \\(A,B\\), supported …", "statement": "<p>**Nonzero finite convolution — original Lemma 2.2.**</p><p>For every integer \\(K\\ge0\\) and real numbers \\(D\\ge\\delta&gt;0\\), there exists\n\\[\n\\varepsilon(K,D,\\delta)&gt;0\n\\]\nsuch that complex Laurent polynomials \\(A,B\\), supported in \\([-K,K]\\), with all coefficients of modulus at most \\(D\\) and\n\\[\n|A_0|,|B_0|\\ge\\delta,\n\\]\nsatisfy\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le n\\le2K}|(AB)_{-n}|\n\\ge\\varepsilon(K,D,\\delta).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The admissible polynomial pairs form a nonempty compact set. For each pair, the first nonzero exponents of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are at most zero, and their sum belongs to \\([-2K,0]\\). The corresponding product coefficient is nonzero.</p><p>The displayed maximum is continuous and positive on this compact set, so it has a positive minimum.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Parameters for the prescribed rotation — original Proposition 2.3.** Fix irrational \\(\\theta\\in(0,1)\\), let \\( X=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,\\qquad \\sigma x=x+\\theta, \\) and let \\(\\mu\\) be Haar probability measure.", "statement": "<p>**Parameters for the prescribed rotation — original Proposition 2.3.**</p><p>Fix irrational \\(\\theta\\in(0,1)\\), let\n\\[\nX=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,\\qquad \\sigma x=x+\\theta,\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mu\\) be Haar probability measure.</p><p>For each \\(j\\), let \\(O_j\\) be the open arc of radius \\(1/(10j)\\) about zero. Fix continuous functions \\(\\varphi_{j,n}\\ge0\\), \\(0\\le n\\le h_j^*\\), such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=0}^{h_j^*}\\varphi_{j,n}=1,\\qquad\n\\sigma^n(\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi_{j,n})\\subset O_j.\n\\]\nSuch functions are obtained from a bump supported in \\(O_j\\), finitely many positive-orbit translates having positive sum, and normalization by that sum.</p><p>There exist positive integers \\(m_j,K_j,G_j,q_i\\), positive real numbers \\(L_j,C_j,\\varepsilon_j\\), and consecutive blocks\n\\[\nJ_j=\\{d_{j-1}+1,\\ldots,d_j\\},\\qquad |J_j|=m_j,\\qquad d_0=0,\n\\]\nwith the following properties. Put\n\\[\nq_0=1,\\quad u_i(x)=\\{q_ix\\},\\quad \\alpha_i=\\{q_i\\theta\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa_j=(100j)^{-1},\\quad N_j=m_jK_j,\\quad\na_j=G_j/m_j,\\quad b_j=m_j^{-19/20},\\quad\nQ_j=m_j^{(7/10)m_j}.\n\\]</p><p>The frequencies are strictly increasing and divisible, and, for \\(i\\in J_j\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1{N_j}&lt;\\alpha_i&lt;\\frac2{N_j},\\qquad\nq_i\\mid q_{i+1},\\qquad\nq_{i+1}/q_i\\ge N_j.\n\\]</p><p>For every integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n\\[\nN_j/4\\le|k|\\le m_j^{(4/5)m_j},\n\\]\nat least \\(m_j/20\\) indices in \\(J_j\\) satisfy\n\\[\n1-\\{k\\alpha_i\\}\\in[b_j,1-b_j].\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\nG_jK_j\\le|k|\\le N_j/4,\n\\]\nevery index in \\(J_j\\) satisfies\n\\[\n1-\\{k\\alpha_i\\}\\in[a_j,1-a_j].\n\\]</p><p>The initial values are \\(K_1=100\\), \\(G_1=1\\). The \\(L_j\\ge1\\) are nondecreasing,\n\\[\nC_j=\\max(L_j,K_j^2),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nm_j\\ge\\max(K_j,2^j,4G_j,200),\\qquad\nK_{j+1}=\\left\\lceil m_j^{(3/4)m_j}\\right\\rceil&gt;2K_j,\n\\]\n\\[\nG_{j+1}K_{j+1}&lt;m_j^{(4/5)m_j},\\qquad\n\\kappa_jm_j^{1/40}\\ge L_j,\\qquad\n\\kappa_{j+1}\\sqrt{G_{j+1}}\\ge L_j,\n\\]\n\\[\nh_{j+1}^*/K_{j+1}\\le2^{-j}.\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nD_j=\\exp\\!\\left((K_j+1)\\sum_{s&lt;j}C_sm_s\\right).\n\\]\nIf \\(A,B\\) are supported in \\([-K_j,K_j]\\), their coefficients have modulus at most \\(D_j\\), and \\(|A_0|,|B_0|\\ge1/j\\), then\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le n\\le2K_j}|(AB)_{-n}|\\ge\\varepsilon_j,\n\\qquad\n(2K_j+1)3e^{-L_j/10000}\\le\\varepsilon_j/j.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, put\n\\[\np_*=d_{j-1},\\qquad\n\\ell_j=\\bigl((j+1)^2(1+p_*)q_{p_*}\\bigr)^{-1}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(t=r/q_{p_*}\\), \\(0\\le r&lt;q_{p_*}\\), there is a finite nonempty set\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_{j,t}\\subset[Q_j/2,Q_j]\\cap\\mathbb N\n\\]\nsuch that \\(\\{n\\theta-t\\}\\in(0,\\ell_j)\\) for all \\(n\\in\\mathcal N_{j,t}\\), and, for \\(r'=1,2\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\n\\frac1{|\\mathcal N_{j,t}|}\n\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_{j,t}}\n\\prod_{i\\in J_j}w_{m_j}^{(r')}(u_i(x),\\{n\\alpha_i\\})\n-1\n\\right\\|_{L^2(\\mu)}\n&lt;\\frac1j.\n\\]\nThe kernels are those of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p>The parameters also satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_{s&gt;j}\\frac1{K_s}\\le\\frac2{K_{j+1}},\\qquad\n\\sum_j\\frac{1+h_j^*}{K_j}&lt;\\infty,\\qquad\n\\frac{Q_j}{K_{j+1}}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(K_j,G_j\\) and all earlier blocks have been fixed. Proposition \\(3\\), with\n\\[\nK=K_j,\\qquad D=D_j,\\qquad \\delta=1/j,\n\\]\ngives \\(\\varepsilon_j\\). Choose \\(L_j\\ge\\max(1,L_{j-1})\\) satisfying the required exponential inequality, omit \\(L_{j-1}\\) at \\(j=1\\), set \\(C_j=\\max(L_j,K_j^2)\\), and choose \\(G_{j+1}\\) with\n\\[\n\\kappa_{j+1}\\sqrt{G_{j+1}}\\ge L_j.\n\\]\nAll remaining scale requirements hold for sufficiently large \\(m=m_j\\). Earlier data, including \\(p_*,q_{p_*},\\ell_j\\) and the finite list of \\(t\\)'s, remain fixed as \\(m\\to\\infty\\). Write\n\\[\nN=mK_j,\\quad Q=m^{(7/10)m},\\quad a=G_j/m,\\quad b=m^{-19/20}.\n\\]</p><p>**Separated powers.** Equidistribution provides at least a fixed positive multiple of \\(\\ell_jQ\\) integers \\(n\\in[Q/2,Q]\\) with \\(\\{n\\theta-t\\}\\in(0,\\ell_j)\\). Put\n\\[\nH=m^{300},\\qquad R=Nm^{1000}.\n\\]\nA greedy selection gives at least \\(m^{0.69m}\\) such powers, separately for every \\(t\\), with\n\\[\n|vn+v'n'|&gt;R\n\\]\nfor distinct selected \\(n,n'\\) and all permitted nonzero frequency pairs.</p><p>Indeed, for \\(v'\\ne0\\), one previously selected \\(n\\) excludes at most \\(2R+2\\) integers \\(n'\\) for each frequency pair. There are at most \\((2H+1)^2\\) pairs and \\(N\\le m^2\\), so only a polynomial factor is lost. If \\(v'=0\\), then \\(|vn|\\ge Q/2&gt;R\\) for large \\(m\\).</p><p>**Trial phases.** Take independent \\(\\lambda_i\\), \\(i\\in J_j\\), uniform on \\([1/N,2/N]\\). For\n\\[\nN/4\\le|k|\\le m^{(4/5)m},\n\\]\nthe law of \\(\\{k\\lambda_i\\}\\) has density at most \\(5\\). Thus one index fails the strict interior version of the spread requirement with probability at most \\(10b\\). Failure at a fixed \\(k\\) has probability at most\n\\[\n2^m(10b)^{(19/20)m}.\n\\]\nA union bound over both signs and the stated range tends to zero, since the coefficient of \\(m\\log m\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac45-\\left(\\frac{19}{20}\\right)^2&lt;0.\n\\]\nFor the lower range,\n\\[\nG_jK_j\\le|k|\\le N/4\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na\\le|k|\\lambda_i\\le1/2,\n\\]\nso no probabilistic choice is needed.</p><p>**Product sampling test.** Fix one orientation \\(u=w_m^{(r')}\\). Put\n\\[\n\\bar u(s)=\\int_0^1u(s,v)\\,dv,\\qquad\nA_*=\\int_0^1\\bar u(s)^2\\,ds.\n\\]\nProposition \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|\\bar u\\|_\\infty\\le2,\\quad\n(1-2m^{-2})^2\\le A_*\\le1+4h^2,\\quad\n\\iint u^2\\le4h^{-1}\\log m,\n\\]\nwhere \\(h=m^{-3/5}\\). Uniformly in \\(s\\), for distinct selected powers,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[u(s,\\{n\\lambda_i\\})u(s,\\{n'\\lambda_i\\})]\n=\\bar u(s)^2+O(m^{-50}).\n\\]\nThe bounded-variation estimate actually gives \\(O(m^{-60})\\). The one-power density estimate similarly gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[u(s,\\{n\\lambda_i\\})\\bar u(s)]\n=\\bar u(s)^2+O(m^{-50}).\n\\]</p><p>For independent Haar variables \\(z_i\\), and a selected power set of size \\(P\\), define\n\\[\nZ(z)=\\frac1P\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_{j,t}}\n\\prod_{i\\in J_j}u(z_i,\\{n\\lambda_i\\}),\n\\qquad\nZ^{\\mathrm{id}}(z)=\\prod_{i\\in J_j}\\bar u(z_i).\n\\]\nIndependence over \\(i\\) makes the distinct-power and cross terms products of \\(m\\) factors \\(A_*+O(m^{-50})\\). By telescoping they equal\n\\[\nA_*^m+O(m^{-49}),\n\\]\nbecause \\((1+4h^2+O(m^{-50}))^m\\) stays bounded. Equal-power terms contribute at most\n\\[\nP^{-1}(8h^{-1}\\log m)^m.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|Z-Z^{\\mathrm{id}}\\|_2^2\n\\le O(m^{-49})+P^{-1}(8h^{-1}\\log m)^m\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nsince \\(P\\ge m^{0.69m}\\) and \\(h^{-1}=m^{0.60}\\). Also\n\\[\n\\|Z^{\\mathrm{id}}-1\\|_2^2\n=A_*^m-2(1-2m^{-2})^m+1\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nusing \\(mh^2=m^{-1/5}\\to0\\).</p><p>There are finitely many \\(t\\)'s. Markov's inequality, both orientations, and the spread estimate therefore give trial phases satisfying all sampling norms with bound \\(1/(2j)\\), together with strict spread inequalities. Avoid the finitely many null events \\(n\\lambda_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\) and the interval endpoints.</p><p>**Realization by circle characters.** Successively choose \\(q_i\\) as multiples of \\(q_{i-1}\\), with \\(\\{q_i\\theta\\}\\) as close to \\(\\lambda_i\\) as desired and \\(q_i/q_{i-1}\\) as large as desired. Positive multiples of \\(q_{i-1}\\theta\\) are dense.</p><p>Along finite-block choices with all new ratios tending to infinity and \\(\\alpha_i\\to\\lambda_i\\), the Haar law of \\((u_i(x))_{i\\in J_j}\\) converges weakly to product Haar measure. For a fixed nonzero integer character vector, its last nonzero coefficient times its frequency eventually dominates all preceding terms, so its Fourier coefficient is eventually zero. Trigonometric approximation proves the weak convergence.</p><p>For fixed \\(m\\), the finitely many squared sampling tests are bounded functions whose discontinuity sets have zero limiting measure: the selected phases avoid cuts, and only finitely many coordinate values lie on the remaining boundaries. Continuous upper and lower approximation outside neighborhoods of these null sets therefore gives convergence of their integrals.</p><p>Choose a sufficiently late realization. Its sampling norms are below \\(1/j\\), its strict angle and spread inequalities persist, and all frequency ratios determined so far meet their bounds. The connecting ratio to the preceding block meets that block's outstanding condition; the final ratio is imposed at the next stage.</p><p>The three final consequences follow from \\(K_{j+1}&gt;2K_j\\), the bound on \\(h_{j+1}^*/K_{j+1}\\), and \\(7/10&lt;3/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Measurable operator fields — original Lemma 3.1.** Let \\((Y,\\nu)\\) be a probability space and \\(K=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Measurable operator fields — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>Let \\((Y,\\nu)\\) be a probability space and \\(K=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)\\). Suppose\n\\[\nA\\in B(L^2(Y,\\nu;K))\n\\]\ncommutes with every scalar multiplier\n\\[\n(M_g\\zeta)(y)=g(y)\\zeta(y),\\qquad g\\in L^\\infty(Y,\\nu).\n\\]\nThen there is a field \\(A(y)\\in B(K)\\), unique almost everywhere, such that\n\\[\n(A\\zeta)(y)=A(y)\\zeta(y),\\qquad\n\\|A(y)\\|\\le\\|A\\|\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Every map \\(y\\mapsto A(y)v\\), \\(v\\in K\\), is measurable.</p><p>Products and adjoints are computed fiberwise. In particular, an orthogonal projection is represented by orthogonal projections outside one null set.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(B\\in B(L^2(Y,\\nu))\\) commutes with all multipliers, put \\(b=B1\\). For every measurable \\(E\\),\n\\[\nB\\mathbf1_E=\\mathbf1_Eb,\\qquad\n\\int_E|b|^2\\,d\\nu\\le\\|B\\|^2\\nu(E).\n\\]\nThus \\(|b|\\le\\|B\\|\\) almost everywhere. The identity \\(Bf=bf\\) follows for simple functions and then by density.</p><p>Apply this to the matrix entries of \\(A\\), obtaining measurable functions \\(a_{ij}\\). For finitely supported vectors \\(v\\) with rational real and imaginary parts, commutation with \\(M_{\\mathbf1_E}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\int_E\\sum_i\\left|\\sum_j a_{ij}(y)v_j\\right|^2\\,d\\nu(y)\n\\le\\|A\\|^2\\|v\\|^2\\nu(E).\n\\]\nThe integrand is therefore at most \\(\\|A\\|^2\\|v\\|^2\\) almost everywhere. Outside a countable union of null sets, the matrix defines an operator \\(A(y)\\) of norm at most \\(\\|A\\|\\). Its columns, and then its values on every vector, are measurable by finite-coordinate approximation.</p><p>The represented field agrees with \\(A\\) on finite-coordinate simple functions, hence everywhere. Applying two represented operators to constant basis fields proves uniqueness. Products act fiberwise; the adjoint has entries \\(\\overline{a_{ji}}\\). The identities \\(p=p^*=p^2\\) give the projection assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The circle representation and strict central diagonals.** Fix irrational \\(\\theta\\), let \\(X=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\), \\(\\sigma x=x+\\theta\\), and let \\(\\mu\\) be Haar probability measure.", "statement": "<p>**The circle representation and strict central diagonals.**</p><p>Fix irrational \\(\\theta\\), let \\(X=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\), \\(\\sigma x=x+\\theta\\), and let \\(\\mu\\) be Haar probability measure. On\n\\[\nH=L^2(X,\\mu;\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)),\n\\]\ndefine\n\\[\nU(x)=S,\\qquad\n\\pi(a)(x)e_k=a(\\sigma^kx)e_k,\\qquad\nV=\\pi(x\\mapsto e^{2\\pi ix}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(Se_k=e_{k+1}\\).</p><p>Then \\(M=W^*(U,V)\\) identifies with the tracial representation of \\(R_\\theta\\), contains \\(\\pi(L^\\infty(X))\\), and consists of measurable fields satisfying\n\\[\nA(\\sigma x)=S^{-1}A(x)S\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Its trace is\n\\[\n\\tau(A)=\\int_X\\langle A(x)e_0,e_0\\rangle\\,d\\mu(x).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(f\\in L^\\infty(X)\\) is positive almost everywhere and a projection \\(p\\in M\\) satisfies\n\\[\n(1-p)U\\pi(f)p=0,\\qquad 0&lt;\\tau(p)&lt;1,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n0&lt;\\langle p(x)e_0,e_0\\rangle&lt;1\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>The operators satisfy \\(VU=e^{2\\pi i\\theta}UV\\). The vectors\n\\[\nU^mV^ne_0:x\\mapsto e^{2\\pi inx}e_m\n\\]\nspan a dense subspace and have the prescribed tracial moments. The commutation relation reduces products of monomials to scalar multiples of monomials, so these moments identify the representation with the stated tracial representation. Measurable functional calculus for \\(V\\) puts every \\(\\pi(a)\\) in \\(M\\).</p><p>Every generator commutes with scalar multiplication in \\(x\\). Proposition \\(5\\) therefore gives the field representation. Every generator also commutes with\n\\[\n(J\\zeta)(x)=S\\zeta(\\sigma x).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\pi(a)\\), this is the coordinate identity\n\\[\na(\\sigma^{k-1}(\\sigma x))=a(\\sigma^kx).\n\\]\nTesting \\(AJ=JA\\) on constant basis fields gives covariance. The trace is the vector state at \\(e_0\\), hence has the displayed integral formula.</p><p>Irrational rotation is ergodic by Fourier coefficients. Lemma \\(6.1\\) now gives the strict-diagonal assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, in its general ergodic setting", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\sigma\\) be an invertible ergodic probability-preserving transformation of \\((Y,\\nu)\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\sigma\\) be an invertible ergodic probability-preserving transformation of \\((Y,\\nu)\\). On \\(L^2(Y,\\nu;\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z))\\), define \\(U\\) and \\(\\pi\\) as in Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p>Suppose a von Neumann algebra \\(M\\) containing \\(U\\) and \\(\\pi(L^\\infty(Y))\\) consists of measurable fields with covariance\n\\[\nA(\\sigma y)=S^{-1}A(y)S\n\\]\nand has faithful normal tracial state\n\\[\n\\tau(A)=\\int_Y\\langle A(y)e_0,e_0\\rangle\\,d\\nu(y).\n\\]\nIf \\(f\\in L^\\infty(Y)\\) is positive almost everywhere,\n\\[\nT=U\\pi(f),\\qquad (1-p)Tp=0,\\qquad 0&lt;\\tau(p)&lt;1,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n0&lt;\\langle p(y)e_0,e_0\\rangle&lt;1\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nr(y)=\\langle p(y)e_0,e_0\\rangle,\\qquad\nD_y=\\operatorname{diag}_k f(\\sigma^ky).\n\\]\nInvariance and covariance give\n\\[\nD_y\\operatorname{ran}p(y)\\subset\\operatorname{ran}p(\\sigma y).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(r(\\sigma y)=0\\), every vector in the latter range has zeroth coordinate zero. Since\n\\[\n(D_yv)_0=f(y)v_0,\\qquad f(y)&gt;0,\n\\]\nevery vector in the former range also has zeroth coordinate zero, so \\(r(y)=0\\).</p><p>If \\(r(y)=1\\), then \\(e_0\\) belongs to the former range; its image \\(f(y)e_0\\) shows that \\(r(\\sigma y)=1\\).</p><p>Thus\n\\[\n\\sigma^{-1}\\{r=0\\}\\subset\\{r=0\\},\\qquad\n\\{r=1\\}\\subset\\sigma^{-1}\\{r=1\\}\n\\]\nmodulo null sets. Probability preservation makes these equalities modulo null sets. Ergodicity makes each event null or conull. The trace formula and \\(0&lt;\\tau(p)&lt;1\\) exclude both conull cases.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Compact-set transfer — original Lemma 3.3.** Let \\(X\\) be a compact metric space with Borel probability measure \\(\\mu\\).", "statement": "<p>**Compact-set transfer — original Lemma 3.3.**</p><p>Let \\(X\\) be a compact metric space with Borel probability measure \\(\\mu\\). Let \\(p(x)\\) be a Borel field of orthogonal projections on \\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)\\), and let\n\\[\nF_k:X\\to(0,\\infty),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\nbe finite Borel functions.</p><p>Suppose finite positive Borel measures \\(\\beta_l\\) on \\(X^2\\) converge weakly to \\(\\beta\\). Assume:</p><p class=\"li\">both families of marginals of \\(\\beta_l\\) are uniformly absolutely continuous with respect to \\(\\mu\\);</p><p class=\"li\">both marginals of \\(\\beta\\) are absolutely continuous;</p><p class=\"li\">one \\(0&lt;B&lt;\\infty\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  F_k(y)/F_k(x)\\le B\n  \\]\n  for all \\(k\\), almost everywhere under every \\(\\beta_l\\) and under \\(\\beta\\).</p><p>If\n\\[\n(1-p(y))\\operatorname{diag}_k\\!\\left(F_k(y)/F_k(x)\\right)p(x)=0\n\\]\nholds \\(\\beta_l\\)-almost everywhere for every \\(l\\), it holds \\(\\beta\\)-almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cap each diagonal entry at \\(B\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), simultaneous Lusin approximation supplies a compact \\(Q\\subset X\\) on which every \\(F_k\\) and every column \\(x\\mapsto p(x)e_k\\) is continuous, while \\(\\mu(X\\setminus Q)\\) is small enough that every \\(\\beta_l\\) gives mass below \\(\\varepsilon\\) to \\(X^2\\setminus Q^2\\).</p><p>The simultaneous assertion follows by applying Lusin's property to the countably many maps with summable exceptional measures and intersecting the resulting compact sets. Lusin's property for separable-metric-valued maps follows from finite-valued approximation and measure regularity.</p><p>On \\(Q^2\\), the capped diagonal field is strongly continuous: continuity on basis vectors and its uniform bound imply continuity on every vector. The same holds for the projection fields, and products of uniformly bounded strongly continuous fields are strongly continuous.</p><p>Consequently, the zero set \\(Z_Q\\subset Q^2\\) of the capped identity is compact. The assumed identity gives\n\\[\n\\beta_l(Z_Q)\\ge\\beta_l(X^2)-\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThe compact-set upper bound for weak convergence and convergence of total masses imply\n\\[\n\\beta(Z_Q)\\ge\\limsup_l\\beta_l(Z_Q)\\ge\\beta(X^2)-\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\varepsilon\\) is arbitrary, the capped identity holds almost everywhere under \\(\\beta\\). Its zero set is measurable by countably many matrix entries. The cap changes no diagonal entry \\(\\beta\\)-almost everywhere.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The measurable circle model and rational endpoint identities — including original Lemma 3.4.** Use the prescribed rotation and parameters of Proposition \\(4\\), and the representation \\(M\\) of Proposition \\(6\\).", "statement": "<p>**The measurable circle model and rational endpoint identities — including original Lemma 3.4.**</p><p>Use the prescribed rotation and parameters of Proposition \\(4\\), and the representation \\(M\\) of Proposition \\(6\\). Define\n\\[\ng_*(x)=\\sum_jC_j\\sum_{i\\in J_j}\\lfloor u_i(x)+\\alpha_i\\rfloor,\\qquad\nf_*(x)=e^{-g_*(x)},\\qquad\nT_*=U\\pi(f_*).\n\\]\nFor \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\), define\n\\[\nF_k(x)=\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\sum_jC_j\\sum_{i\\in J_j}\n\\lfloor u_i(x)+k\\alpha_i\\rfloor\\right).\n\\]</p><p>These sums have finitely many nonzero terms almost everywhere. On an invariant conull set, all \\(F_k\\) are positive and finite,\n\\[\nF_0=1,\\qquad F_1=f_*,\\qquad\nF_{v+n}(x)=F_v(x)F_n(\\sigma^vx),\n\\]\nand \\(F_k(x)\\) is nonincreasing in \\(k\\). Give the functions arbitrary positive finite Borel values off this set, and write\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(k)=F_k(y)/F_k(x).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(u_i(x)\\le u_i(y)\\), coordinate \\(i\\in J_s\\) contributes at most \\(1\\) to this ratio and contributes \\(e^{-C_s}\\) precisely when\n\\[\nu_i(x)&lt;1-\\{k\\alpha_i\\}\\le u_i(y).\n\\]\nWithout order, one coordinate contributes at most \\(e^{C_s}\\).</p><p>For every invariant projection \\(p\\in M\\),\n\\[\n(1-p)T_*p=0,\n\\]\nthe positive-orbit identity\n\\[\n(1-p(\\sigma^nx))\\operatorname{diag}_vR_{x,\\sigma^nx}(v)p(x)=0\n\\]\nholds almost everywhere for every \\(n\\ge1\\).</p><p>Moreover, for every \\(A\\ge1\\) and every integer multiple \\(t\\) of \\(1/q_A\\),\n\\[\n(1-p(x+t))\\operatorname{diag}_vR_{x,x+t}(v)p(x)=0\n\\]\nfor almost every \\(x\\). Exceptional sets may be chosen simultaneously for this countable family.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\mu\\{\\lfloor u_i+\\alpha_i\\rfloor\\ne0\\}\n=\\sum_i\\alpha_i\n\\le2\\sum_jK_j^{-1}&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nthe first Borel–Cantelli implication gives \\(0&lt;f_*\\le1\\) almost everywhere. For fixed \\(k\\), in the tail where \\(|k|\\alpha_i&lt;1\\), the probability of a nonzero floor is \\(|k|\\alpha_i\\). Thus every \\(F_k\\) is positive and finite almost everywhere. Take a countable intersection and all integer translates.</p><p>The floor addition identity and\n\\[\nu_i(\\sigma^vx)=\\{u_i(x)+v\\alpha_i\\}\n\\]\nprove the cocycle formula term by term. Positivity of the \\(\\alpha_i\\) gives monotonicity. The floor difference for ordered coordinates is either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), with the stated crossing criterion. For arbitrary coordinates its absolute value is at most \\(1\\).</p><p>Invariance under \\(T_*\\) implies invariance under every positive power. Since\n\\[\nT_*(x)^n=S^n\\operatorname{diag}_vF_n(\\sigma^vx),\n\\]\ncovariance gives the orbit identity after division by \\(F_n(x)&gt;0\\), because\n\\[\nF_n(\\sigma^vx)/F_n(x)=F_v(\\sigma^nx)/F_v(x).\n\\]\nThis diagonal is bounded on each fiber by \\(1/F_n(x)\\), since \\(F_n\\le1\\).</p><p>Fix \\(A,t\\). At every sufficiently late stage \\(j\\), put \\(a=d_{j-1}\\ge A\\). Divisibility makes \\(t\\) an integer multiple of \\(1/q_a\\), so the sampling set \\(\\mathcal N_{j,t}\\) occurs in Proposition \\(4\\).</p><p>Average the graph measures \\(x\\mapsto(x,\\sigma^nx)\\), \\(n\\in\\mathcal N_{j,t}\\), with starting densities\n\\[\n\\prod_{i\\in J_j}w_{m_j}(u_i(x),\\{n\\alpha_i\\}).\n\\]\nBoth marginal densities tend to \\(1\\) in \\(L^2\\), using the second orientation for the endpoint marginal. Since every translation \\(n\\theta\\) lies within \\(\\ell_j\\) of \\(t\\), these measures converge weakly to the Haar-first graph measure of \\(x\\mapsto x+t\\).</p><p>At positive density,\n\\[\n0&lt;\\{n\\alpha_i\\}&lt;m_j^{-3/5}\\Delta(u_i(x)),\n\\qquad i\\in J_j,\n\\]\nso current-block coordinates advance without wrap.</p><p>For every selected \\(n\\), remove the same starting set:\n\\[\nu_i(x)\\ge1-q_a\\ell_j\\quad(A\\le i\\le a),\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nu_i(x)\\ge1-2Q_j/N_s\n\\quad(i\\in J_s,\\ s&gt;j).\n\\]\nIts Haar measure is at most\n\\[\naq_a\\ell_j+2Q_j\\sum_{s&gt;j}K_s^{-1}\n\\le(j+1)^{-2}+4Q_j/K_{j+1}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe averaged first marginal is uniformly \\(L^2\\)-bounded, so Cauchy–Schwarz shows that removed weighted mass tends to zero. Trimmed measures have the same weak limit, and their marginals remain dominated by uniformly \\(L^2\\)-bounded marginals.</p><p>For \\(A\\le i\\le a\\), \\(q_it\\) is integral, and the coordinate advance is\n\\[\nq_i\\{n\\theta-t\\}&lt;q_a\\ell_j,\n\\]\nwithout wrap after trimming. In future blocks it is \\(n\\alpha_i&lt;2Q_j/N_s\\), again without wrap. Thus every coordinate \\(i\\ge A\\) is ordered. Consequently,\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(v)\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_{i&lt;A}C_{s(i)}\\right),\n\\qquad i\\in J_{s(i)},\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(j,v\\). The same bound holds on the limiting graph, because \\(u_i(x+t)=u_i(x)\\) for \\(i\\ge A\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition \\(7\\) to the positive-orbit identities. Countability gives simultaneous exceptional sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Densities of independent coordinates — original Lemma 4.1.** Let \\((\\Omega_i,\\mathcal F_i,\\mu_i)\\), \\(i\\ge1\\), be probability spaces.", "statement": "<p>**Densities of independent coordinates — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><p>Let \\((\\Omega_i,\\mathcal F_i,\\mu_i)\\), \\(i\\ge1\\), be probability spaces. Suppose \\(h_i\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\int h_i\\,d\\mu_i=1,\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\|h_i-1\\|_{L^2(\\mu_i)}^2\\le C&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\nu=\\bigotimes_{i\\ge1}(h_i\\mu_i)\n\\]\nhas a density relative to \\(\\mu=\\bigotimes_i\\mu_i\\) of \\(L^2(\\mu)\\)-norm at most \\(e^{C/2}\\).</p><p>In particular, if two densities \\(h_i^0,h_i^1\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\|h_i^a-1\\|_2^2\\le b_i\\quad(a=0,1),\\qquad\n\\sum_i b_i\\le C,\n\\]\nthe same bound holds for every deterministic coordinatewise selection \\(h_i^{\\epsilon_i}\\), \\(\\epsilon_i\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The mean-one condition gives\n\\[\n\\|h_i\\|_2^2=1+\\|h_i-1\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\prod_{i=1}^nh_i\\right\\|_2^2\n=\\prod_{i=1}^n(1+\\|h_i-1\\|_2^2)\n\\le e^C.\n\\]\nIntegration under \\(\\nu\\), initially on bounded cylinder functions, is therefore bounded by \\(e^{C/2}\\|\\cdot\\|_2\\). Cylinder functions are dense in \\(L^2(\\mu)\\), so the functional extends continuously. Riesz representation gives \\(H\\in L^2(\\mu)\\) of norm at most \\(e^{C/2}\\).</p><p>The finite signed measure \\(H\\mu\\) and \\(\\nu\\) agree on cylinder sets. Uniqueness on the product sigma-algebra gives \\(H\\mu=\\nu\\), so \\(H\\ge0\\) and \\(\\int H\\,d\\mu=1\\). Apply this argument to \\(h_i^{\\epsilon_i}\\) for the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Ordered digit couplings — original Proposition 4.3, with the marginal and crossing estimates of Lemmas 4.2 and 4.4.** Use the circle parameters of Proposition \\(4\\).", "statement": "<p>**Ordered digit couplings — original Proposition 4.3, with the marginal and crossing estimates of Lemmas 4.2 and 4.4.**</p><p>Use the circle parameters of Proposition \\(4\\). Put\n\\[\nr_i^*=q_{i+1}/q_i.\n\\]\nA Haar point has the mixed-radix expansion\n\\[\nx=\\frac{A_0}{q_1}+\\sum_{i\\ge1}\\frac{A_i}{q_{i+1}},\n\\]\nwith independent uniform digits\n\\[\nA_0\\in\\{0,\\ldots,q_1-1\\},\\qquad\nA_i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,r_i^*-1\\}.\n\\]\nOutside ambiguous endpoints,\n\\[\nA_i=\\lfloor r_i^*u_i(x)\\rfloor.\n\\]</p><p>For each block index \\(j\\), define a labelled coupling law \\(\\mathbb P_j\\) as follows. Use a common uniform initial digit. Independently for \\(i\\in J_s\\), choose a raw pair \\((X_i,Y_i)\\). For each labelled width \\(w=a_s,b_s\\), put\n\\[\n\\rho_s(w)=\\kappa_s\\sqrt{w/m_s},\n\\]\nand include:</p><p class=\"li\">a top component of probability \\(\\rho_s(w)\\), with independent uniforms\n  \\[\n  X_i\\in[0,1-w/2),\\qquad Y_i\\in[1-w/2,1);\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">a bottom component of probability \\(\\rho_s(w)\\), with independent uniforms\n  \\[\n  X_i\\in[0,w/2),\\qquad Y_i\\in[w/2,1).\n  \\]</p><p>If \\(s=j\\), also include a tag of probability \\(1/m_s\\), with independent uniforms\n\\[\nX_i\\in[0,1/(2m_s)),\\qquad\nY_i\\in[1-1/(2m_s),1).\n\\]\nWith the remaining probability, set \\(X_i=Y_i\\) to a uniform point. The remainder is positive. Keep width labels distinct even if the widths coincide.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nA_i=\\lfloor r_i^*X_i\\rfloor,\\qquad\nB_i=\\lfloor r_i^*Y_i\\rfloor,\n\\]\nand form endpoints \\(x,y\\) from their digits.</p><p>There is \\(H_0&lt;\\infty\\), independent of \\(j\\), such that both endpoint densities, and every deterministic digitwise hybrid endpoint density, have \\(L^2(\\mu)\\)-norm at most \\(H_0\\). Almost surely,\n\\[\nu_i(x)\\le u_i(y),\\qquad\n|u_i(x)-X_i|,\\ |u_i(y)-Y_i|\\le1/r_i^*.\n\\]</p><p>Conditional on \\(x\\), coordinate labels are independent and each posterior depends only on \\(A_i\\). Conditional on \\(y\\), the corresponding statement holds with \\(B_i\\). If \\(i\\in J_s\\), \\(w=a_s\\) or \\(b_s\\), and \\(u\\in[w,1-w]\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(\\text{top label of width }w\\text{ at }i\\mid x)\n\\ge\\rho_s(w)/10\n\\quad\\text{if }u_i(x)&lt;u,\n\\]\nand this label forces \\(u_i(y)\\ge u\\). Similarly,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(\\text{bottom label of width }w\\text{ at }i\\mid y)\n\\ge\\rho_s(w)/10\n\\quad\\text{if }u_i(y)\\ge u,\n\\]\nand this label forces \\(u_i(x)&lt;u\\).</p><p>For the functions \\(F_k\\) of Proposition \\(8\\), every projection \\(p\\in M\\) satisfying \\((1-p)T_*p=0\\) obeys\n\\[\n(1-p(y))\\operatorname{diag}_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}R_{x,y}(k)p(x)=0\n\\]\nfor \\(\\mathbb P_j\\)-almost every pair, and\n\\[\n0&lt;R_{x,y}(k)\\le1\n\\]\nsimultaneously for all \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Specifying a finite mixed-radix prefix selects an interval of reciprocal product length, proving the independent-uniform digit description. The parameter bounds\n\\[\n\\rho_s(w)/w\\le\\kappa_s,\\qquad w\\le1/4,\\qquad m_s\\ge200\n\\]\ngive a positive mixture remainder.</p><p>Every raw pair satisfies \\(X_i\\le Y_i\\), hence \\(A_i\\le B_i\\). The nonnegative tail expansion gives coordinate order and the rounding estimates. Lemmas \\(10.1\\) and \\(10.2\\) prove the marginal and conditional assertions.</p><p>Let \\(y^{(D)}\\) use \\(y\\)'s digits at indices \\(1,\\ldots,D\\) and \\(x\\)'s later digits. Then\n\\[\nu_i(x)\\le u_i(y^{(D)}),\\qquad\ny^{(D)}\\to y,\\qquad\ny^{(D)}-x\\in q_{D+1}^{-1}\\mathbb Z/\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(D\\), only finitely many translations occur. Proposition \\(8\\) holds off a common Haar-null set for these translations, and absolute continuity of the \\(x\\)-marginal gives the pair identity at \\((x,y^{(D)})\\), even though the translation depends on the pair.</p><p>Coordinate order bounds all ratios by \\(1\\), both before and after passage to the limit. The pair laws converge weakly by almost-sure convergence. Lemma \\(10.1\\) bounds both marginal families in \\(L^2\\); a set of Haar measure \\(\\delta\\) has mass at most \\(H_0\\sqrt\\delta\\). Proposition \\(7\\), with cap \\(B=1\\), transfers the identity to \\((x,y)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform marginal bounds — original Lemma 4.2.** For the digit coupling of Proposition \\(10\\), both endpoint laws and every deterministic coordinatewise hybrid endpoint law have Haar densities of \\(L^2\\)-norm at most …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform marginal bounds — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><p>For the digit coupling of Proposition \\(10\\), both endpoint laws and every deterministic coordinatewise hybrid endpoint law have Haar densities of \\(L^2\\)-norm at most\n\\[\nH_0,\\qquad\nH_0^2=\\exp\\!\\left(50\\left(\\sum_{s\\ge1}\\kappa_s^2+1\\right)\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Either raw marginal density is at most \\(10\\): for each width, top and bottom contributions together are at most \\(4\\rho_s(w)/w\\), the tag contributes at most \\(2\\), and the diagonal component contributes at most \\(1\\).</p><p>A uniform density on an interval of length at least \\(w/2\\) differs from \\(1\\) by a mean-zero function of squared \\(L^2\\)-norm at most \\(2/w\\). For a tagged interval, the bound is \\(2m_s\\). Express the raw marginal minus \\(1\\) as at most five weighted mean-zero perturbations. Finite-sum Cauchy–Schwarz gives, for \\(i\\in J_s\\),\n\\[\n\\int_0^1d_i(t)^2\\,dt\n\\le1+50\\left(\\frac{\\kappa_s^2}{m_s}\n+\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\{s=j\\}}}{m_s}\\right).\n\\]\nThe four width terms contribute at most \\(40\\kappa_s^2/m_s\\), and the tag at most \\(10/m_s\\).</p><p>Passing to digits averages \\(d_i\\) on radix bins; Jensen's inequality preserves this estimate. Relative to independent uniform digits, the coordinate densities have mean one. Summing their squared deviations gives\n\\[\n50\\left(\\sum_s\\kappa_s^2+1\\right),\n\\]\nfor either endpoint and every deterministic hybrid. Proposition \\(9\\) gives the bound \\(H_0\\).</p><p>Outside ambiguous expansions, the mixed-radix map is a measure-preserving bijection from the reference digit space to \\(X\\), so transporting the density preserves its \\(L^2\\)-norm.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Conditional labels and forced crossings — original Lemma 4.4.** The conditional independence and posterior lower bounds stated in Proposition \\(10\\) hold under the full labelled law \\(\\mathbb P_j\\), before restriction …", "statement": "<p>**Conditional labels and forced crossings — original Lemma 4.4.**</p><p>The conditional independence and posterior lower bounds stated in Proposition \\(10\\) hold under the full labelled law \\(\\mathbb P_j\\), before restriction to any further event.</p>", "proof": "<p>The entire endpoint determines its digits almost surely. For a finite collection of labels, conditioning on all \\(x\\)-digits gives the product of individual posteriors\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbb P_j(\\text{label }\\ell\\text{ at }i)\n      \\mathbb P_j(A_i=a\\mid\\text{label }\\ell\\text{ at }i)}\n     {\\mathbb P_j(A_i=a)}.\n\\]\nVerify this first against digit-cylinder tests using independence of coordinate pairs, then against all bounded digit-measurable tests by the monotone class theorem. The common initial digit is independent. This proves conditional independence; the argument for \\(y\\) is identical.</p><p>The parameters give\n\\[\n1/r_i^*\\le1/N_s&lt;w/2.\n\\]\nIf \\(u_i(x)&lt;u\\le1-w\\), the whole radix bin containing \\(X_i\\) lies in \\([0,1-w/2)\\). The top component has \\(X_i\\)-density at least \\(1\\) there, and the total raw marginal density is at most \\(10\\). Its posterior probability is therefore at least \\(\\rho_s(w)/10\\). Under that label,\n\\[\nu_i(y)\\ge1-w/2-1/r_i^*&gt;1-w\\ge u.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(u_i(y)\\ge u\\ge w\\), the entire \\(Y_i\\)-bin lies in \\([w/2,1)\\). The bottom component again has density at least \\(1\\), so its posterior is at least \\(\\rho_s(w)/10\\). It forces\n\\[\nu_i(x)\\le w/2+1/r_i^*&lt;w\\le u.\n\\]\nThese estimates include thresholds at radix-bin endpoints.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Conditional convolution obstruction — original Lemma 5.1.** Let \\(K\\ge1\\) be an integer, \\(D\\ge a>0\\), \\(c>0\\), and \\(\\eta,\\delta\\ge0\\).", "statement": "<p>**Conditional convolution obstruction — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>Let \\(K\\ge1\\) be an integer, \\(D\\ge a&gt;0\\), \\(c&gt;0\\), and \\(\\eta,\\delta\\ge0\\). On a probability space with endpoints \\(x,y\\), let \\(\\xi(x),\\gamma(y)\\) be measurable vectors in the unit ball of \\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)\\). Let \\(F_k\\) be positive finite measurable endpoint functions, with \\(F_0=1\\), and put\n\\[\nA_v=\\frac{\\xi_v(x)}{F_v(x)},\\qquad\nB_w=F_{-w}(y)\\gamma_{-w}(y),\\qquad\nR(k)=\\frac{F_k(y)}{F_k(x)}.\n\\]\nSuppose, almost surely, for \\(0\\le n\\le2K\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{v\\in\\mathbb Z}A_vB_{-n-v}=0,\n\\]\nand, for every \\(v\\),\n\\[\n0&lt;\\frac{F_{n+v}(y)}{F_v(x)}\n\\le\\min\\{R(v),R(n+v)\\}\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(E\\) have probability at least \\(c\\), and suppose on \\(E\\),\n\\[\n|A_v|,|B_v|\\le D\\quad(|v|\\le K),\\qquad\n|A_0|,|B_0|\\ge a.\n\\]\nFor each \\(|k|&gt;K\\), suppose events \\(H_k^{(1)},H_k^{(2)}\\) satisfy\n\\[\nR(k)\\le\\eta+\\mathbf1_{H_k^{(1)}}+\\mathbf1_{H_k^{(2)}}\\quad\\text{on }E,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(H_k^{(1)}\\mid x)\\le\\delta,\\qquad\n\\mathbb P(H_k^{(2)}\\mid y)\\le\\delta\n\\]\nunder the full law, before restriction to \\(E\\).</p><p>Then, for the constant of Proposition \\(3\\),\n\\[\nc\\,\\varepsilon(K,D,a)\\le(2K+1)(\\eta+2\\sqrt\\delta).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The multiplier bound and Cauchy–Schwarz make each convolution absolutely convergent. Put\n\\[\nc_n=\\sum_{\\substack{|v|\\le K\\\\|n+v|\\le K}}A_vB_{-n-v},\n\\qquad 0\\le n\\le2K.\n\\]\nOn \\(E\\), Proposition \\(3\\) applied to the truncated Laurent polynomials gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=0}^{2K}|c_n|\\ge\\varepsilon(K,D,a).\n\\]\nIts expectation on \\(E\\) is at least \\(c\\varepsilon(K,D,a)\\).</p><p>For an omitted index \\(v\\), choose deterministically\n\\[\nk(v)=\n\\begin{cases}\nv,&amp;|v|&gt;K,\\\\\nn+v,&amp;|v|\\le K.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThen \\(|k(v)|&gt;K\\). The zero convolution and the two multiplier bounds give\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_E|c_n|\n\\le\\eta+\n\\sum_{b=1}^2\\sum_{v\\text{ omitted}}\n|\\xi_v(x)\\gamma_{n+v}(y)|\\mathbf1_{H_{k(v)}^{(b)}}.\n\\]\nThe unmasked sum of coordinate products is at most one. The indicator \\(\\mathbf1_E\\) has been dropped from the nonnegative exceptional terms so that full-law conditional bounds apply.</p><p>For the first exceptional sum, Cauchy–Schwarz on probability times counting measure bounds its expectation by\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\text{ omitted}}\n|\\xi_v(x)|^2\\mathbf1_{H_{k(v)}^{(1)}}\n\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{v\\text{ omitted}}|\\gamma_{n+v}(y)|^2\n\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le\\sqrt\\delta.\n\\]\nCondition on \\(x\\) in the first factor and use \\(\\sum_v|\\xi_v(x)|^2\\le1\\). For the other sum, place the indicator with \\(|\\gamma_{n+v}(y)|^2\\) and condition on \\(y\\), obtaining the same bound.</p><p>Tonelli's theorem justifies all nonnegative interchanges. Taking expectations and summing over \\(n\\) proves the assertion. No sum of individual exceptional probabilities is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The measurable circle obstruction — original Theorem 5.2.** For the fixed irrational rotation, parameters, and measurable weight of Propositions \\(4\\), \\(6\\), and \\(8\\), the bounded operator \\( T_*=U\\pi(f_*) \\) has no …", "statement": "<p>**The measurable circle obstruction — original Theorem 5.2.**</p><p>For the fixed irrational rotation, parameters, and measurable weight of Propositions \\(4\\), \\(6\\), and \\(8\\), the bounded operator\n\\[\nT_*=U\\pi(f_*)\n\\]\nhas no projection \\(p\\in M\\) satisfying\n\\[\n0&lt;\\tau(p)&lt;1,\\qquad (1-p)T_*p=0.\n\\]</p><p>For the contradiction argument, if such \\(p\\) existed, write\n\\[\nr(x)=p(x)_{0,0},\\qquad\n\\xi(x)=p(x)e_0,\\qquad\n\\gamma(y)=(1-p(y))e_0.\n\\]\nUse the coupling laws \\(\\mathbb P_j\\) of Proposition \\(10\\), and their expectations \\(\\mathbb E_j\\). The scalar coefficient from \\(\\gamma\\) in an inner product is conjugated; equivalently, take\n\\[\nA_v=\\xi_v(x)/F_v(x),\\qquad\nB_w=F_{-w}(y)\\overline{\\gamma_{-w}(y)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the stated projection exists. Proposition \\(6\\) gives \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\) almost everywhere. Proposition \\(10\\) puts\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diag}_vR_{x,y}(v)\\xi(x)\n\\]\nin \\(\\operatorname{ran}p(y)\\). Apply \\(T_*(y)^n\\), \\(n\\ge0\\), and pair with \\(\\gamma(y)\\). The cocycle identity gives\n\\[\n0=\n\\sum_{v\\in\\mathbb Z}\n\\frac{F_{n+v}(y)}{F_v(x)}\n\\xi_v(x)\\overline{\\gamma_{n+v}(y)}\n=\\sum_{v\\in\\mathbb Z}A_vB_{-n-v}.\n\\]\nAll identities hold simultaneously under each coupling.</p><p>Monotonicity and coordinate order give\n\\[\n0&lt;\n\\frac{F_{n+v}(y)}{F_v(x)}\n\\le\\min\\{R_{x,y}(v),R_{x,y}(n+v)\\}\\le1.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz makes the convolutions absolutely convergent.</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(j\\), apply Proposition \\(11\\), using Lemmas \\(12.1\\) and \\(12.2\\), with\n\\[\nK=K_j,\\quad D=D_j,\\quad a=1/j,\\quad c=c_*,\n\\quad \\eta=e^{-L_j},\\quad\\delta=e^{-L_j/400}.\n\\]\nIt yields\n\\[\nc_*\\varepsilon_j\n\\le(2K_j+1)(e^{-L_j}+2e^{-L_j/800})\n\\le\\varepsilon_j/j\n\\]\nby the parameter choice. Since \\(\\varepsilon_j&gt;0\\), this is impossible once \\(1/j&lt;c_*\\). Thus no such projection exists.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive event and finite convolution — original Lemma 5.3.** Under the hypothetical nontrivial invariant projection of Proposition \\(12\\), put \\(K=K_j\\), \\(m=m_j\\).", "statement": "<p>**Positive event and finite convolution — original Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>Under the hypothetical nontrivial invariant projection of Proposition \\(12\\), put \\(K=K_j\\), \\(m=m_j\\). Let \\(E_j\\) be the event\n\\[\nX_i&lt;1-3/m,\\quad Y_i&gt;3/m\\quad(i\\in J_j),\n\\]\nwith at least one tag in \\(J_j\\),\n\\[\nX_i&lt;1-3K/N_s,\\quad Y_i&gt;3K/N_s\n\\quad(i\\in J_s,\\ s&gt;j),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nr(x)\\ge1/j,\\qquad 1-r(y)\\ge1/j.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nc_{j,n}\n=\\sum_{\\substack{|v|\\le K_j\\\\|n+v|\\le K_j}}\nA_vB_{-n-v},\n\\qquad 0\\le n\\le2K_j.\n\\]</p><p>There is a constant \\(c_*&gt;0\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(E_j)\\ge c_*,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_j\\!\\left[\n\\mathbf1_{E_j}\\sum_{n=0}^{2K_j}|c_{j,n}|\n\\right]\\ge c_*\\varepsilon_j.\n\\]\nThe threshold in \\(j\\) may depend on \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At one current-block coordinate, a tag has probability \\(1/m\\) and satisfies both endpoint exclusions. The raw marginal density bound \\(10\\) makes inadmissibility have probability at most \\(60/m\\). A nontagged admissible outcome therefore has probability at least \\(1-61/m\\).</p><p>Independence gives a lower bound for exactly one tag and admissible nontags elsewhere:\n\\[\nm\\frac1m(1-61/m)^{m-1}\\ge e^{-90},\n\\qquad m\\ge200.\n\\]\nThis follows from \\(\\log(1-t)\\ge-t/(1-t)\\).</p><p>Future exclusions lose at most\n\\[\n60K_j\\sum_{s&gt;j}K_s^{-1}\n\\le120K_j/K_{j+1}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nBy Proposition \\(6\\), \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\) Haar-almost everywhere. Proposition \\(10\\) bounds the last-condition losses by\n\\[\nH_0\\mu\\{r&lt;1/j\\}^{1/2}\n+H_0\\mu\\{1-r&lt;1/j\\}^{1/2}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus \\(c_*=e^{-90}/2\\) works eventually.</p><p>On \\(E_j\\), rounding changes raw coordinates by at most \\(1/N_s\\), so\n\\[\nu_i(x)&lt;1-2K/N_s,\\qquad u_i(y)&gt;2K/N_s\n\\quad(i\\in J_s,\\ s\\ge j).\n\\]\nFor \\(0\\le v\\le K\\), the floors of \\(u_i(x)+v\\alpha_i\\) and \\(u_i(y)-v\\alpha_i\\) vanish in these blocks. Earlier-block floors have absolute value at most \\(K+1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n1/F_v(x)\\le D_j,\\qquad F_{-v}(y)\\le D_j.\n\\]\nFor \\(v&lt;0\\), both quantities are at most \\(1\\), by monotonicity.</p><p>All coefficients of\n\\[\nA^{[K]}(z)=\\sum_{|v|\\le K}A_vz^v,\\qquad\nB^{[K]}(z)=\\sum_{|v|\\le K}B_vz^v\n\\]\nhave modulus at most \\(D_j\\). Their constant coefficients are \\(r(x)\\) and \\(1-r(y)\\), each at least \\(1/j\\). Their coefficient of \\(z^{-n}\\) is \\(c_{j,n}\\). Proposition \\(4\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=0}^{2K}|c_{j,n}|\\ge\\varepsilon_j\n\\]\non \\(E_j\\). Integration proves the second assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Conditional attenuation of every omitted index — original Lemma 5.4.** Under the setting of Proposition \\(12\\), for every \\(j\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(|k|>K_j\\), there are measurable events …", "statement": "<p>**Conditional attenuation of every omitted index — original Lemma 5.4.**</p><p>Under the setting of Proposition \\(12\\), for every \\(j\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(|k|&gt;K_j\\), there are measurable events \\(H_k^{(1)},H_k^{(2)}\\) on the labelled coupling space such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(H_k^{(1)}\\mid x)\\le e^{-L_j/400},\\qquad\n\\mathbb P_j(H_k^{(2)}\\mid y)\\le e^{-L_j/400}\n\\]\nalmost surely under the full law, and\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(k)\n\\le e^{-L_j}+\\mathbf1_{H_k^{(1)}}+\\mathbf1_{H_k^{(2)}}\n\\]\non \\(E_j\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First let \\(K_j&lt;|k|\\le N_j/4\\). A tagged coordinate satisfies, after rounding,\n\\[\nu_i(x)&lt;1/m_j,\\qquad u_i(y)&gt;1-1/m_j.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\beta=|k|\\alpha_i\\in(1/m_j,1/2).\n\\]\nThe threshold is \\(1-\\beta\\) for \\(k&gt;0\\) and \\(\\beta\\) for \\(k&lt;0\\); it is crossed in either case. Thus\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(k)\\le e^{-C_j}\\le e^{-L_j}\n\\]\non \\(E_j\\). Take both exceptional events empty.</p><p>Put \\(M_s=m_s^{(4/5)m_s}\\). The intervals\n\\[\n[N_j/4,M_j],\\qquad [G_sK_s,M_s]\\quad(s&gt;j)\n\\]\ncover \\([N_j/4,\\infty)\\): successive intervals overlap by the parameter inequalities, their right endpoints tend to infinity, and \\(G_sK_s\\le N_s/4\\).</p><p>For each remaining \\(k\\), choose deterministically a block \\(J_s\\), a width \\(w=a_s\\) or \\(b_s\\), and \\(I\\subset J_s\\), \\(|I|\\ge m_s/20\\), such that\n\\[\nu_i^*=1-\\{k\\alpha_i\\}\\in[w,1-w],\\qquad i\\in I.\n\\]\nThe lower-range choice has \\(s&gt;j\\). In either case,\n\\[\nm_s\\rho_s(w)\\ge L_j,\\qquad C_s\\ge L_j.\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\nm_s\\rho_s(b_s)=\\kappa_sm_s^{1/40}\\ge L_s,\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\nm_s\\rho_s(a_s)=\\kappa_s\\sqrt{G_s}\\ge L_{s-1}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(H_k\\) be the event that none of the listed thresholds is crossed. Split it into\n\\[\nH_k^{(1)}\n=H_k\\cap\\{\\#\\{i\\in I:u_i(x)&lt;u_i^*\\}\\ge|I|/2\\},\n\\qquad\nH_k^{(2)}=H_k\\setminus H_k^{(1)}.\n\\]\nGiven \\(x\\), the first event forbids the corresponding top labels at at least \\(|I|/2\\) indices. Conditional independence and Proposition \\(10\\) give\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(H_k^{(1)}\\mid x)\n\\le(1-\\rho_s(w)/10)^{|I|/2}\n\\le e^{-m_s\\rho_s(w)/400}\n\\le e^{-L_j/400}.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(H_k\\), order and absence of crossings give\n\\[\nu_i(x)&lt;u_i^*\\iff u_i(y)&lt;u_i^*.\n\\]\nThus \\(H_k^{(2)}\\) requires more than \\(|I|/2\\) indices with \\(u_i(y)\\ge u_i^*\\). Given \\(y\\), the corresponding bottom labels are forbidden. The reverse conditional estimate gives the same exponential bound.</p><p>Outside \\(H_k\\), at least one coordinate crosses, and every coordinate is ordered. Hence\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(k)\\le e^{-C_s}\\le e^{-L_j}.\n\\]\nOn \\(H_k\\), the ratio is at most \\(1\\), proving the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Transport by an affiliated positive operator — original Lemma 6.1.** Let \\((M,\\tau)\\) be a finite von Neumann algebra acting on a Hilbert space, with faithful normal tracial state.", "statement": "<p>**Transport by an affiliated positive operator — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><p>Let \\((M,\\tau)\\) be a finite von Neumann algebra acting on a Hilbert space, with faithful normal tracial state. Let \\(H\\) be an injective positive self-adjoint operator affiliated with \\(M\\), and let \\(A,B\\in M\\). Suppose\n\\[\nA(\\operatorname{Dom}H)\\subset\\operatorname{Dom}H,\\qquad\nHA\\zeta=BH\\zeta\\quad(\\zeta\\in\\operatorname{Dom}H).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(p\\in M\\) is a projection with \\(A\\operatorname{ran}p\\subset\\operatorname{ran}p\\), then the projection \\(P\\) onto\n\\[\n\\overline{H(\\operatorname{ran}p\\cap\\operatorname{Dom}H)}\n\\]\nbelongs to \\(M\\), is invariant under \\(B\\), and satisfies\n\\[\n\\tau(P)=\\tau(p).\n\\]\nAffiliation means that all spectral projections of \\(H\\) belong to \\(M\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every commutant unitary preserves \\(\\operatorname{ran}p\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Dom}H\\), and commutes with \\(H\\) there. It therefore maps the displayed closed space onto itself. Its projection commutes with the commutant, so \\(P\\in M\\).</p><p>If \\(\\zeta\\in\\operatorname{ran}p\\cap\\operatorname{Dom}H\\), both invariance assumptions give\n\\[\nA\\zeta\\in\\operatorname{ran}p\\cap\\operatorname{Dom}H,\\qquad\nBH\\zeta=HA\\zeta.\n\\]\nThus \\(B\\) preserves the unclosed image, and boundedness extends this to its closure.</p><p>For trace preservation, put\n\\[\nE_N=\\mathbf1_{[1/N,N]}(H).\n\\]\nInjectivity gives \\(E_N\\uparrow1\\). Let \\(q_N=p\\wedge E_N\\). The bounded element\n\\[\nHq_N=(HE_N)q_N\\in M\n\\]\nis bounded below by \\(1/N\\) on its initial space. Its image is closed, and its polar decomposition identifies \\(q_N\\) with its final projection, which is dominated by \\(P\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\tau(P)\\ge\\tau(p\\wedge E_N)\n\\ge\\tau(p)+\\tau(E_N)-1.\n\\]\nThe last inequality follows from the polar decomposition of \\((1-E_N)p\\), whose initial support is \\(p-(p\\wedge E_N)\\) and final support is at most \\(1-E_N\\). Passing to the limit gives \\(\\tau(P)\\ge\\tau(p)\\).</p><p>For the reverse inequality, set\n\\[\nq_N'=(1-p)\\wedge E_N.\n\\]\nThe bounded operator\n\\[\nH^{-1}q_N'=(H^{-1}E_N)q_N'\n\\]\nis bounded below on its initial space and has closed image. This image is orthogonal to \\(\\operatorname{ran}P\\), since for \\(\\eta\\in\\operatorname{ran}q_N'\\) and \\(\\zeta\\in\\operatorname{ran}p\\cap\\operatorname{Dom}H\\),\n\\[\n\\langle H^{-1}\\eta,H\\zeta\\rangle\n=\\langle\\eta,\\zeta\\rangle=0.\n\\]\nHere \\(H^{-1}\\eta\\in\\operatorname{Dom}H\\). Polar decomposition and the same meet estimate give\n\\[\n\\tau(1-P)\\ge\\tau((1-p)\\wedge E_N)\n\\ge\\tau(1-p)+\\tau(E_N)-1.\n\\]\nLet \\(N\\to\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Continuous one-zero weight, measurable coboundary, and norm estimate — Sections 6.2–6.4.** Use the fixed circle rotation, parameters, and measurable cost \\(g_*\\) of Propositions \\(4\\) and \\(8\\).", "statement": "<p>**Continuous one-zero weight, measurable coboundary, and norm estimate — Sections 6.2–6.4.**</p><p>Use the fixed circle rotation, parameters, and measurable cost \\(g_*\\) of Propositions \\(4\\) and \\(8\\).</p><p>There exist a nonnegative function \\(g\\), finite and continuous on \\(X\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) and tending to infinity at \\(0\\), and an almost-everywhere finite real measurable function \\(b\\), such that\n\\[\ng=g_*+1+b\\circ\\sigma-b\n\\]\nalmost everywhere and\n\\[\n\\int_Xg\\,d\\mu=\\infty.\n\\]\nSet \\(g(0)=\\infty\\) and\n\\[\nf(e^{2\\pi ix})=e^{-g(x)},\\qquad e^{-\\infty}=0.\n\\]\nThen \\(f:\\mathbb T\\to[0,1]\\) is continuous,\n\\[\nf^{-1}(\\{0\\})=\\{1\\},\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathbb T}\\log f\\,dm=-\\infty,\n\\]\nand \\(T_f=U\\pi(f)\\) is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent.</p><p>Multiplication in fiber coordinate \\(k\\) by \\(e^{b(\\sigma^kx)}\\), with its natural square-integrability domain, defines an injective positive self-adjoint operator \\(H_b\\) affiliated with \\(M\\), and\n\\[\nT_f(\\operatorname{Dom}H_b)\\subset\\operatorname{Dom}H_b,\\qquad\nH_bT_f=e^{-1}T_*H_b\n\\]\non \\(\\operatorname{Dom}H_b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\ng=g_0+\\sum_i h_i'\\quad\\text{off }0,\\qquad g(0)=\\infty,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nb=b_0-\\sum_i(d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}+d_i').\n\\]\nLemmas \\(14.1\\) and \\(14.2\\) give all regularity, positivity, divergence, and integral assertions. On the common conull set where the original cost and all transfer sums at \\(x,\\sigma x\\) have finite support, sum their identities to obtain\n\\[\ng=g_*+1+b\\circ\\sigma-b.\n\\]\nNo integrability of \\(b\\) is asserted or needed.</p><p>The definition of \\(f\\) gives continuity at its unique zero as well as away from it, and\n\\[\n\\int\\log f\\,dm=-\\int g\\,d\\mu=-\\infty.\n\\]\nThe spectral distribution of \\(\\pi(f)\\) is the Haar distribution of \\(f\\), so the Fuglede–Kadison determinant is zero. Since \\(|U\\pi(f)|=\\pi(f)\\), the same holds for \\(T_f\\).</p><p>Choose a finite Borel version of \\(b\\) and remove all integer translates of the null set where the coboundary identity fails. The spectral projections of \\(H_b\\) are \\(\\pi\\)-images of spectral indicators of \\(e^b\\), so \\(H_b\\) is affiliated with \\(M\\), positive, self-adjoint, and injective. The identity gives\n\\[\ne^{b(\\sigma^{k+1}x)}e^{-g(\\sigma^kx)}\n=e^{-1}f_*(\\sigma^kx)e^{b(\\sigma^kx)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\zeta\\in\\operatorname{Dom}H_b\\), the right side is the \\((k+1)\\)-coordinate of \\(e^{-1}T_*H_b\\zeta\\), a square-integrable vector. Thus \\(T_f\\zeta\\in\\operatorname{Dom}H_b\\) and the intertwining relation holds.</p><p>For the norm estimate, let\n\\[\n\\ell_M(x)=\\log\\max(f(e^{2\\pi ix}),e^{-M}).\n\\]\nIt is continuous, and its integrals decrease to \\(-\\infty\\). Irrational rotation averages of continuous functions converge uniformly to their integrals, first by geometric sums for Fourier monomials and then by uniform trigonometric approximation.</p><p>Given \\(a&gt;0\\), choose \\(M\\) with \\(\\int\\ell_M\\,d\\mu&lt;-2a\\). For all sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\sup_x\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}f(e^{2\\pi i\\sigma^kx})\n\\le\n\\sup_x\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\ell_M(\\sigma^kx)\\right)\n\\le e^{-an}.\n\\]\nThe left side equals \\(\\|T_f^n\\|\\), since the power is a unitary times multiplication by this continuous product. The spectral-radius formula gives radius zero because \\(a\\) is arbitrary. Finally, \\(f&gt;0\\) almost everywhere, so \\(T_f\\ne0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Smoothing and moving logarithmic drops — Section 6.2.** For \\(i\\in J_j\\), put \\(\\delta_i=\\alpha_i/4\\) and define the continuous periodic smoothing \\( s_i(u)=\\frac1{2\\delta_i}\\int_{-\\delta_i}^{\\delta_i}\\{u+t\\}\\,dt. \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Smoothing and moving logarithmic drops — Section 6.2.**</p><p>For \\(i\\in J_j\\), put \\(\\delta_i=\\alpha_i/4\\) and define the continuous periodic smoothing\n\\[\ns_i(u)=\\frac1{2\\delta_i}\\int_{-\\delta_i}^{\\delta_i}\\{u+t\\}\\,dt.\n\\]\nThere are continuous nonnegative functions \\(h_i'\\), supported in \\(O_j\\), and real measurable transfers \\(d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}},d_i'\\), such that\n\\[\nh_i'\n=C_j\\lfloor u_i+\\alpha_i\\rfloor\n+d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}+d_i'\n-(d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}+d_i')\\circ\\sigma,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_Xh_i'\\,d\\mu=C_j\\alpha_i,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\bigl(\n\\mu\\{d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}\\ne0\\}+\\mu\\{d_i'\\ne0\\}\n\\bigr)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThus the transfer sums are finite almost everywhere. The sum \\(\\sum_i h_i'\\) is locally finite and continuous on \\(X\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), and has infinite integral.</p>", "proof": "<p>The smoothing equals \\(\\{u\\}\\) outside the \\(\\delta_i\\)-neighborhood of the cut. Define\n\\[\nh_i(x)=C_j\\bigl(\\alpha_i+s_i(u_i(x))-s_i(u_i(x)+\\alpha_i)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\n\\lfloor\\{u\\}+\\alpha_i\\rfloor\n=\\alpha_i+\\{u\\}-\\{u+\\alpha_i\\}\n\\]\nshows that \\(h_i\\) is an average of the original one-coordinate cost. Therefore it is nonnegative and continuous,\n\\[\n\\int h_i\\,d\\mu=C_j\\alpha_i,\\qquad\n\\mu(\\operatorname{supp}h_i)\\le\\alpha_i+2\\delta_i.\n\\]</p><p>For\n\\[\nd_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}(x)\n=C_j(s_i(u_i(x))-u_i(x)),\n\\]\n\\[\nh_i=C_j\\lfloor u_i+\\alpha_i\\rfloor\n+d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}-d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}\\circ\\sigma.\n\\]\nIts transfer support has measure at most \\(2\\delta_i\\).</p><p>Using the partition of unity from Proposition \\(4\\), define\n\\[\nh_i'(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{h_j^*}(h_i\\varphi_{j,n})(\\sigma^{-n}x).\n\\]\nThis is continuous, nonnegative, supported in \\(O_j\\), and has the same integral as \\(h_i\\). Telescoping gives\n\\[\nh_i'=h_i+d_i'-d_i'\\circ\\sigma,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nd_i'(x)=\n\\sum_{n=1}^{h_j^*}\\sum_{r=1}^n\n(h_i\\varphi_{j,n})(\\sigma^{-r}x).\n\\]\nAlthough this is a double sum, its support lies in \\(h_j^*\\) translates of \\(\\operatorname{supp}h_i\\), one per possible \\(r\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\bigl(\n\\mu\\{d_i^{\\mathrm{sm}}\\ne0\\}+\\mu\\{d_i'\\ne0\\}\n\\bigr)\n\\le C\\sum_j\\frac{1+h_j^*}{K_j}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nBorel–Cantelli gives almost-everywhere finiteness, also at the translated point.</p><p>Successive blocks of \\(h_i'\\) are supported in \\(O_j\\downarrow\\{0\\}\\), so their sum is locally finite away from zero. Tonelli and \\(\\alpha_i&gt;1/(m_jK_j)\\) give\n\\[\n\\int_X\\sum_i h_i'\\,d\\mu\n=\\sum_jC_j\\sum_{i\\in J_j}\\alpha_i\n\\ge\\sum_jC_j/K_j=\\infty,\n\\]\nsince \\(C_j\\ge K_j^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Background cost diverging at zero — Section 6.3.** There exist \\(g_0\\), finite and continuous on \\(X\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), and an almost-everywhere finite real measurable \\(b_0\\), such that …", "statement": "<p>**Background cost diverging at zero — Section 6.3.**</p><p>There exist \\(g_0\\), finite and continuous on \\(X\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), and an almost-everywhere finite real measurable \\(b_0\\), such that\n\\[\ng_0\\ge1/2,\\qquad g_0(x)\\to\\infty\\quad(x\\to0),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_Xg_0\\,d\\mu&lt;\\infty,\\qquad\ng_0=1+b_0\\circ\\sigma-b_0\n\\]\nalmost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(W_j=2^{j+1}\\). Choose continuous \\(0\\le v_j\\le1\\), with \\(v_j(0)=1\\), supported in closed arcs \\(I_j\\downarrow\\{0\\}\\), small enough that\n\\[\n\\sigma I_j,\\ldots,\\sigma^{W_j}I_j\n\\]\nare pairwise disjoint and\n\\[\n\\sum_jW_j\\mu(I_j)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nFinite irrational orbit segments have positive minimum separation, so this is possible.</p><p>For \\(x\\ne0\\), put\n\\[\ng_0(x)=1+\\sum_j\\left(\nv_j(x)-\\frac1{W_j}\\sum_{n=1}^{W_j}v_j(\\sigma^{-n}x)\n\\right).\n\\]\nDisjointness bounds the norm of the \\(j\\)-th subtracted function by \\(1/W_j\\), and these bounds sum to \\(1/2\\). The negative series converges uniformly; the positive series is locally finite away from zero. Thus \\(g_0\\) is continuous there and at least \\(1/2\\).</p><p>For every fixed \\(J\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\le J}v_j(x)\\to J\n\\quad(x\\to0).\n\\]\nThe bounded negative series implies divergence at zero. The integral is finite because\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int v_j\\,d\\mu\\le\\sum_j\\mu(I_j)&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nand the negative series is uniformly bounded.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nb_0(x)=\\sum_j\\frac1{W_j}\n\\sum_{n=1}^{W_j}\\sum_{r=1}^n v_j(\\sigma^{-r}x).\n\\]\nThe \\(j\\)-th summand is supported in\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{r=1}^{W_j}\\sigma^rI_j.\n\\]\nThe summability assumption makes the sum finite almost everywhere. Where the sums at \\(x\\) and \\(\\sigma x\\) are finite, telescoping gives the coboundary identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Product scales, random angles, and phase ranges — Section A.1 and original Proposition A.2.** All notation in this proposition and subsequent product propositions is local to the product model.", "statement": "<p>**Product scales, random angles, and phase ranges — Section A.1 and original Proposition A.2.**</p><p>All notation in this proposition and subsequent product propositions is local to the product model.</p><p>Partition \\(\\mathbb N\\) into consecutive blocks \\(J_j\\) of lengths \\(m_j\\), and put\n\\[\nd_0=0,\\qquad d_j=\\sum_{s\\le j}m_s,\\qquad\n\\kappa_j=(100j)^{-1},\\qquad K_1=100,\\quad G_1=1.\n\\]\nThe following recursive choices are feasible.</p><p>At stage \\(j\\), define\n\\[\nD_j=\\exp\\!\\left((K_j+1)\\sum_{s&lt;j}C_sm_s\\right),\n\\]\nchoose \\(\\varepsilon_j&gt;0\\) from Proposition \\(3\\) for\n\\[\n(K,D,\\delta)=(K_j,D_j,1/j),\n\\]\nchoose nondecreasing \\(L_j\\ge1\\) with\n\\[\n(2K_j+1)3e^{-L_j/10000}\\le\\varepsilon_j/j,\n\\]\nset \\(C_j=\\max(L_j,K_j^2)\\), and choose \\(G_{j+1}\\ge1\\) with\n\\[\n\\kappa_{j+1}\\sqrt{G_{j+1}}\\ge L_j.\n\\]\nChoose \\(m=m_j\\) sufficiently large and put\n\\[\nK_{j+1}=\\lceil m^{(3/4)m}\\rceil,\n\\]\nsubject to\n\\[\nm\\ge\\max(K_j,2^j,4G_j,100),\\qquad d_{j-1}\\le m/100,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa_jm^{1/40}\\ge L_j,\\qquad\nG_{j+1}K_{j+1}&lt;m^{(4/5)m}.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nN_j=m_jK_j,\\qquad a_j=G_j/m_j,\\qquad b_j=m_j^{-19/20}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nd_j\\le1.01m_j,\\quad N_j\\le m_j^2,\\quad\nK_{j+1}&gt;2K_j,\\quad\n\\sum_{s&gt;j}K_s^{-1}\\le2/K_{j+1},\n\\]\n\\[\n1/m_j\\le a_j,b_j\\le1/4.\n\\]</p><p>Choose independent random angles \\(\\theta_i\\), uniform on \\([1/N_j,2/N_j]\\) for \\(i\\in J_j\\). For every realization,\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\theta_i\\le2\\sum_jK_j^{-1}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nAlmost surely, \\(1,\\theta_1,\\theta_2,\\ldots\\) are rationally independent, and \\(\\sigma_\\theta x=x+\\theta\\) is free and ergodic on \\(X=\\mathbb T^{\\mathbb N}\\).</p><p>Almost surely, for every sufficiently large \\(j\\), simultaneously for all integers in the stated ranges:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(G_jK_j\\le|k|\\le N_j/4\\), then every \\(i\\in J_j\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   1-\\{k\\theta_i\\}\\in[a_j,1-a_j].\n   \\]\n   This assertion holds at every stage for every realization.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(N_j/4\\le|k|\\le m_j^{(4/5)m_j}\\), at least \\(m_j/20\\) coordinates satisfy\n   \\[\n   1-\\{k\\theta_i\\}\\in[b_j,1-b_j].\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The choices of \\(\\varepsilon_j,L_j,C_j,G_{j+1}\\) precede \\(m_j\\). All but the final size requirement are lower bounds on \\(m\\). For the last,\n\\[\n\\frac{G_{j+1}\\lceil m^{(3/4)m}\\rceil}{m^{(4/5)m}}\n\\le2G_{j+1}m^{-m/20}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus there is no conflicting upper restriction. The displayed consequences follow from the scale requirements and a geometric majorant for reciprocal \\(K_j\\)'s.</p><p>For rational independence, clear denominators in a proposed finite relation. If an angle has nonzero coefficient, conditioning on all other angles specifies one value of an atomless variable, an event of probability zero. There are countably many nontrivial integer relations.</p><p>Finite-support characters form an orthonormal basis of \\(L^2(X,\\mu)\\). Translation multiplies the character indexed by \\(v\\) by\n\\[\ne^{2\\pi i\\sum_i v_i\\theta_i},\n\\]\nwhich equals \\(1\\) only for the constant character. Hence the rotation is ergodic. Irrationality of \\(\\theta_1\\) makes it free.</p><p>For the first phase range,\n\\[\na_j\\le|k|\\theta_i\\le1/2.\n\\]\nThe threshold is \\(1-|k|\\theta_i\\) for \\(k&gt;0\\), and \\(|k|\\theta_i\\) for \\(k&lt;0\\).</p><p>For the second, the periodized law of \\(k\\theta_i\\) has density at most\n\\[\n1+N_j/|k|\\le5.\n\\]\nAn individual coordinate misses \\([b_j,1-b_j]\\) with probability at most \\(10b_j\\). Independence and a union bound over missing sets give failure probability at a fixed \\(k\\) at most\n\\[\n2^{m_j}(10b_j)^{(19/20)m_j}.\n\\]\nA further union bound over both signs and the range gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\text{failure at }j)\n\\le\n2m_j^{(4/5)m_j}2^{m_j}(10b_j)^{(19/20)m_j}\n=\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{41}{400}m_j\\log m_j+O(m_j)\\right).\n\\]\nThese probabilities are summable because \\(m_j\\ge2^j\\). Borel–Cantelli proves eventual simultaneous validity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Ordered orbit-pair sampling — original Proposition A.3.** Use the product scales and random angles of Proposition \\(15\\).", "statement": "<p>**Ordered orbit-pair sampling — original Proposition A.3.**</p><p>Use the product scales and random angles of Proposition \\(15\\). On \\(X=\\mathbb T^{\\mathbb N}\\), write \\(x\\le y\\) for coordinatewise order in representatives \\([0,1)\\).</p><p>For finite \\(I\\subset\\mathbb N\\), let \\(\\lambda_I\\) be the finite measure on \\(X^2\\) using\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{0\\le x_i&lt;y_i&lt;1\\}}\\,dx_i\\,dy_i\n\\]\nin coordinates \\(i\\in I\\), and the uniform diagonal law \\((x_i,x_i)\\) in all other coordinates. Then\n\\[\n\\lambda_I(X^2)=2^{-|I|},\n\\]\nand its first and second marginal densities are respectively\n\\[\n\\prod_{i\\in I}(1-x_i),\\qquad \\prod_{i\\in I}y_i,\n\\]\nboth bounded by \\(1\\).</p><p>With probability one in the angles, for every finite \\(I\\) there are finite positive measures \\(\\eta_{I,l}\\) converging weakly to \\(\\lambda_I\\), each carried by ordered positive-orbit pairs\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x,\\sigma_\\theta^nx)\n\\]\nwith \\(n\\) in a finite set of positive integers. Both marginals have \\(L^2(\\mu)\\) densities bounded uniformly in \\(l\\); the bound may depend on \\(I\\) and on the fixed angle realization.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix finite \\(I\\), and take \\(l\\) large enough to contain \\(I\\) in the first \\(d=d_l\\) coordinates. Write\n\\[\nm=m_l,\\quad Q=m^{(7/10)m},\\quad\nH=m^{300},\\quad R=N_lm^{1000}.\n\\]\nA deterministic greedy selection, as in Proposition \\(4\\), gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_l\\subset[Q/2,Q]\\cap\\mathbb N,\\qquad\nP_l=|\\mathcal N_l|\\ge m^{0.69m},\n\\]\nsuch that distinct selected powers satisfy\n\\[\n|vn+v'n'|&gt;R\n\\]\nfor all permitted nonzero frequency pairs. One previous power excludes only \\(O(m^{1602})\\) integers, whereas there are order \\(Q\\) choices.</p><p>For \\(i\\in J_s\\), \\(s\\le l\\), Proposition \\(2\\), with\n\\[\nN_0=N_s\\le N_l=N\\le m^2,\n\\]\ngives a density \\(\\phi_{i,n}\\) for \\(t_n=\\{n\\theta_i\\}\\) with\n\\[\n\\|\\phi_{i,n}-1\\|_\\infty\\le N_s/n\\le N_l/n.\n\\]\nIt also gives the two-power bounded-variation discrepancy and\n\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E g(t_n)-\\int_0^1g\\right|\n\\le(N_l/n)\\int_0^1|g|.\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\nh=m^{-3/5},\\quad \\alpha=m^{-2},\\quad\n\\Delta(s)=\\min(s,1-s),\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nw_i(s,t)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{0&lt;t&lt;1-s\\}},&amp;i\\in I,\\\\{}[1mm]\n\\displaystyle\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\Delta(s)\\ge\\alpha\\}}\n\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\{0&lt;t&lt;h\\Delta(s)\\}}}{h\\Delta(s)},&amp;i\\notin I.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\widetilde\\eta_{I,l}\\) average the graph measures of \\(x\\mapsto(x,\\sigma_\\theta^nx)\\) with weights\n\\[\nW_n(x)=\\prod_{i\\le d}w_i(x_i,\\{n\\theta_i\\}).\n\\]\nThese weights impose \\(x_i&lt;y_i\\) for \\(i\\le d\\).</p><p>For a cylinder rectangle with rational interval tests \\(E_i^x,E_i^y\\), define the tested kernels in the two marginal orientations:\n\\[\nu_i^{(1)}(s,t)\n=w_i(s,t)\\mathbf1_{E_i^x}(s)\\mathbf1_{E_i^y}(\\{s+t\\}),\n\\]\n\\[\nu_i^{(2)}(s,t)\n=w_i(\\{s-t\\},t)\\mathbf1_{E_i^x}(\\{s-t\\})\n\\mathbf1_{E_i^y}(s).\n\\]\nAll tested coordinates are taken below \\(d\\). Without a test, use full intervals.</p><p>For either orientation, put\n\\[\n\\bar u_i(s)=\\int_0^1u_i(s,t)\\,dt,\\qquad\nA_i=\\int_0^1\\bar u_i(s)^2\\,ds.\n\\]\nUniformly in the coordinate and tests,\n\\[\n\\|u_i(s,\\cdot)\\|_\\infty+\\operatorname{Var}_t u_i(s,\\cdot)\n=O(m^{13/5})=O(m^{10}),\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\le\\bar u_i\\le2,\\qquad\nA_i\\le1+O(h^2),\\qquad\n\\iint u_i^2=O(h^{-1}\\log m).\n\\]\nFor triangular factors, the untested averages are \\(1-s\\) and \\(s\\), total and square integrals are \\(1/2\\), and \\(A_i=1/3\\). For near-diagonal factors these are Proposition \\(2\\). Tests only decrease nonnegative moments. Their translated interval indicators have bounded variation, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(ab)\n\\le\\|a\\|_\\infty\\operatorname{Var}(b)\n+\\|b\\|_\\infty\\operatorname{Var}(a).\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nq_n(z)=\\prod_{i\\le d}u_i(z_i,\\{n\\theta_i\\}),\\qquad\nZ_l=P_l^{-1}\\sum_{n\\in\\mathcal N_l}q_n,\\qquad\nZ_l^0=\\prod_{i\\le d}\\bar u_i.\n\\]\nIndependence across angle coordinates gives exact products for expected pairings. For distinct \\(n,n'\\), each coordinate factor in\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\langle q_n,q_{n'}\\rangle\n\\]\nis \\(A_i+O(m^{-50})\\), by Proposition \\(2\\). The same is true for \\(\\mathbb E\\langle q_n,Z_l^0\\rangle\\), by the one-phase estimate.</p><p>No lower bound on \\(A_i\\) is needed: telescoping gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\prod_{i\\le d}(A_i+e_i)-\\prod_{i\\le d}A_i\\right|\n\\le cdm^{-50}(1+ch^2+cm^{-50})^{d-1}\n=O(dm^{-50}),\n\\]\nbecause \\(d\\le1.01m\\) and \\(dh^2\\to0\\). Self-pairings are bounded by\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|q_n\\|_2^2\\le(ch^{-1}\\log m)^d.\n\\]\nExpansion of the squared norm has main terms\n\\[\n(1-P_l^{-1})A-2A+A=-A/P_l\\le0,\n\\qquad A=\\prod_{i\\le d}A_i.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|Z_l-Z_l^0\\|_2^2\n\\le O(dm^{-40})+P_l^{-1}(ch^{-1}\\log m)^d\n\\le O(m^{-39})+m^{-0.04m}.\n\\]\nFor the last inequality, use \\(d\\le1.01m\\), \\(h^{-1}=m^{3/5}\\), and \\(P_l\\ge m^{0.69m}\\).</p><p>This bound is summable in \\(l\\). Tonelli shows that, almost surely,\n\\[\n\\sum_l\\|Z_l-Z_l^0\\|_2^2&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nfor every fixed finite \\(I\\), orientation, and rational cylinder test. Intersect these countably many probability-one events.</p><p>Without tests, \\(Z_l\\) is a marginal density and \\(\\|Z_l^0\\|_2\\) stays bounded. Thus both marginal densities of \\(\\widetilde\\eta_{I,l}\\) are uniformly \\(L^2\\)-bounded.</p><p>For a fixed rectangle, \\(L^2\\)-convergence reduces its mass to the ideal product model. Triangular factors are exact. Outside \\(I\\), the ideal model takes uniform \\(x_i\\), discards \\(\\Delta(x_i)&lt;\\alpha\\), and takes \\(y_i-x_i\\) uniform in \\((0,h\\Delta(x_i))\\). Total cutoff loss is at most\n\\[\n2d\\alpha\\to0,\n\\]\nand \\(|y_i-x_i|\\le h/2\\). In each fixed tested coordinate, interval membership differs only in an \\(O(h)\\)-neighborhood of endpoints. Hence\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\eta_{I,l}(R)\\to\\lambda_I(R)\n\\]\nfor every rational cylinder rectangle.</p><p>These limits imply weak convergence. Continuous functions on the compact product are uniformly approximable by finite-coordinate continuous functions; on each finite torus, rational-grid step functions approximate uniformly. The total masses are uniformly bounded.</p><p>Finally restrict \\(\\widetilde\\eta_{I,l}\\) to order in every coordinate \\(i&gt;d\\), obtaining \\(\\eta_{I,l}\\). A selected summand's weight depends only on the first \\(d\\) coordinates, while its tail remains independent Haar. For a uniform tail coordinate, order fails with probability\n\\[\n\\{n\\theta_i\\}\\le n\\theta_i.\n\\]\nThe relative discarded mass is therefore at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&gt;d}n\\theta_i\n\\le2m^{(7/10)m}\\sum_{s&gt;l}K_s^{-1}\n\\le4m^{(7/10)m}/K_{l+1}\n\\le4m^{-m/20}\\to0.\n\\]\nThe total discarded mass tends to zero. Trimmed marginals are submeasures of the untrimmed marginals, so their \\(L^2\\)-norms cannot increase. The desired conclusions follow on the one probability-one event.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The product factor, weight, and cocycles.** Fix a realization of the angles for which rational independence and Propositions \\(15\\) and \\(16\\) hold.", "statement": "<p>**The product factor, weight, and cocycles.**</p><p>Fix a realization of the angles for which rational independence and Propositions \\(15\\) and \\(16\\) hold. Write \\(\\sigma x=x+\\theta\\), and define \\(U,\\pi,M\\) as in Theorem \\(4\\).</p><p>Then \\(M\\) is a type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) factor with separable predual. Its elements are precisely the essentially bounded measurable fields \\(A(x)\\) satisfying\n\\[\nA(\\sigma x)=S^{-1}A(x)S\n\\]\nalmost everywhere, and\n\\[\n\\tau(A)=\\int_XA(x)_{0,0}\\,d\\mu(x)\n\\]\nis its faithful normal tracial state.</p><p>Define\n\\[\nf(x)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\sum_jC_j\\sum_{i\\in J_j}\n\\lfloor x_i+\\theta_i\\rfloor\\right),\\qquad T=U\\pi(f),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF_k(x)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\sum_jC_j\\sum_{i\\in J_j}\n\\lfloor x_i+k\\theta_i\\rfloor\\right).\n\\]\nOn an invariant conull Borel set, all sums have finite support, \\(F_k\\) are positive and finite,\n\\[\nF_0=1,\\qquad\nF_{k+n}(x)=F_n(\\sigma^kx)F_k(x),\n\\]\nand \\(F_{k+1}(x)\\le F_k(x)\\). Extend them positively and finitely off that set.</p><p>For ordered endpoints,\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(k)=F_k(y)/F_k(x)\\le1.\n\\]\nCoordinate \\(i\\in J_s\\) contributes \\(e^{-C_s}\\) when\n\\[\nx_i&lt;1-\\{k\\theta_i\\}\\le y_i.\n\\]</p><p>The operator \\(T\\) is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent,\n\\[\n\\int_X\\log f\\,d\\mu=-\\infty,\\qquad \\Delta(T)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(17.1\\) proves the factor assertions.</p><p>Because \\(\\sum_i\\theta_i&lt;\\infty\\), Borel–Cantelli makes the cost defining \\(f\\) finite almost everywhere. For fixed \\(k\\), all sufficiently late coordinates have \\(|k|\\theta_i&lt;1\\), and their nonzero-floor probabilities are \\(|k|\\theta_i\\). Borel–Cantelli, countable intersection over \\(k\\), and all translates give the common invariant conull set.</p><p>The coordinate identity\n\\[\n\\lfloor x_i+(k+n)\\theta_i\\rfloor\n=\n\\lfloor x_i+k\\theta_i\\rfloor\n+\\lfloor\\{x_i+k\\theta_i\\}+n\\theta_i\\rfloor\n\\]\nproves the cocycle law. Monotonicity and the ordered crossing formula follow directly from the floor function, for negative as well as positive \\(k\\).</p><p>Lemma \\(17.2\\) supplies the norm, integral, and determinant conclusions. The norm estimate also gives spectrum \\(\\{0\\}\\) and Brown measure \\(\\delta_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Product factor and field characterization — original Lemma A.4.** For the rationally independent fixed product rotation of Proposition \\(17\\), the algebra \\(M\\), its field characterization, and its faithful normal …", "statement": "<p>**Product factor and field characterization — original Lemma A.4.**</p><p>For the rationally independent fixed product rotation of Proposition \\(17\\), the algebra \\(M\\), its field characterization, and its faithful normal trace have the properties stated there.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every generator commutes with scalar multiplication and with\n\\[\n(J\\zeta)(x)=S\\zeta(\\sigma x).\n\\]\nProposition \\(5\\) gives measurable fields, and commutation with \\(J\\) gives covariance.</p><p>Conversely, let \\(A\\) be a bounded covariant field and put \\(a_r(x)=A(x)_{r,0}\\). Covariance gives\n\\[\nA(x)_{k+r,k}=a_r(\\sigma^kx),\n\\]\nso its \\(r\\)-th diagonal is \\(U^r\\pi(a_r)\\in M\\).</p><p>For constant-field unitaries \\(D_te_k=e^{2\\pi ikt}e_k\\), Fejér averaging gives\n\\[\nA_N=\\int_0^1F_N(t)D_tAD_t^*\\,dt\n=\\sum_{|r|&lt;N}\\left(1-\\frac{|r|}{N}\\right)U^r\\pi(a_r)\\in M.\n\\]\nThe map \\(t\\mapsto D_tAD_t^*\\) is strongly continuous and uniformly bounded. Positivity, unit mass, and concentration of \\(F_N\\) give \\(A_N\\to A\\) strongly. Hence \\(A\\in M\\).</p><p>The trace formula is the vector state at \\(e_0\\), so it is normal. If \\(A\\ge0\\) and \\(\\tau(A)=0\\), then its \\(00\\) entry vanishes almost everywhere. Covariance forces every diagonal entry to vanish. Positivity, or\n\\[\n|A_{k,l}|^2\\le A_{k,k}A_{l,l},\n\\]\ngives \\(A=0\\), proving faithfulness.</p><p>For \\(A,B\\in M\\), covariance and \\(z=\\sigma^rx\\) give\n\\[\n\\int A(x)_{0,r}B(x)_{r,0}\\,d\\mu(x)\n=\n\\int B(z)_{0,-r}A(z)_{-r,0}\\,d\\mu(z).\n\\]\nRow and column \\(\\ell^2\\)-bounds make the sums absolutely integrable. Summing proves traciality.</p><p>A central element commutes with \\(\\pi(e^{2\\pi ix_1})\\), whose fiber diagonal entries are distinct because \\(\\theta_1\\) is irrational. It is therefore diagonal. Commutation with \\(U\\) makes all diagonal entries equal, and covariance plus ergodicity makes their common value constant. Thus the center is scalar.</p><p>The algebra contains the infinite-dimensional abelian algebra \\(\\pi(L^\\infty(X))\\), so the finite factor is not a matrix algebra and is of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\). The Hilbert space is separable; the predual is a quotient of separable trace class.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Product norm, logarithmic integral, and determinant — original Lemma A.5.** For the weight \\(f\\) and operator \\(T\\) of Proposition \\(17\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Product norm, logarithmic integral, and determinant — original Lemma A.5.**</p><p>For the weight \\(f\\) and operator \\(T\\) of Proposition \\(17\\),\n\\[\nT\\ne0,\\qquad\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|T^n\\|^{1/n}=0,\\qquad\n\\int_X\\log f\\,d\\mu=-\\infty,\\qquad\n\\Delta(T)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The cocycle identity gives\n\\[\nT^n=U^n\\pi(F_n).\n\\]\nAll floors defining \\(F_n\\) are nonnegative. For fixed \\(q\\), on the first \\(q\\) blocks,\n\\[\n\\lfloor x_i+n\\theta_i\\rfloor\\ge n\\theta_i-1.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}\\|T^n\\|^{1/n}\n\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\sum_{s\\le q}C_s\\sum_{i\\in J_s}\\theta_i\\right)\n\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left(-\\sum_{s\\le q}C_s/K_s\\right).\n\\]\nSince \\(C_s\\ge K_s^2\\), the final bound tends to zero as \\(q\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Tonelli gives\n\\[\n-\\int_X\\log f\\,d\\mu\n=\\sum_sC_s\\sum_{i\\in J_s}\\theta_i\n\\ge\\sum_sC_s/K_s=\\infty.\n\\]\nSince \\(|T|=\\pi(f)\\), the determinant formula gives \\(\\Delta(T)=0\\). Positivity of \\(f\\) almost everywhere gives \\(T\\ne0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Projection identities under triangular laws — original Proposition A.6.** Use the fixed product model of Proposition \\(17\\) and the measures \\(\\lambda_I\\) of Proposition \\(16\\).", "statement": "<p>**Projection identities under triangular laws — original Proposition A.6.**</p><p>Use the fixed product model of Proposition \\(17\\) and the measures \\(\\lambda_I\\) of Proposition \\(16\\).</p><p>If \\(p\\in M\\) is a projection satisfying\n\\[\n(1-p)Tp=0,\n\\]\nthen, for every finite \\(I\\subset\\mathbb N\\),\n\\[\n(1-p(y))\\operatorname{diag}_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}R_{x,y}(k)p(x)=0\n\\]\nfor \\(\\lambda_I\\)-almost every pair.</p><p>If additionally \\(0&lt;\\tau(p)&lt;1\\), then\n\\[\n0&lt;r(x):=p(x)_{0,0}&lt;1\n\\]\nfor \\(\\mu\\)-almost every \\(x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a Borel projection field and shrink the common invariant conull set so that covariance, cocycles, and all positive-power identities hold. Invariance under \\(T(x)^n\\), followed by \\(S^{-n}\\), gives\n\\[\n(1-p(\\sigma^nx))\n\\operatorname{diag}_kF_n(\\sigma^kx)p(x)=0.\n\\]\nDivision by \\(F_n(x)&gt;0\\) changes the entries to\n\\[\nF_k(\\sigma^nx)/F_k(x).\n\\]</p><p>Proposition \\(16\\) supplies ordered positive-orbit measures converging to \\(\\lambda_I\\), with uniformly \\(L^2\\)-bounded marginal densities. Absolute continuity of their first marginals makes the countably many orbit identities valid almost everywhere. Uniform \\(L^2\\)-bounds give uniform absolute continuity. The limiting marginals are absolutely continuous. Order gives the common ratio cap \\(B=1\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(7\\) proves the triangular-law identity. Finally, the proof of Lemma \\(6.1\\) applies: the product base is ergodic, the trace is faithful with the required formula, and \\(f&gt;0\\) almost everywhere.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Biased product couplings — original Proposition A.8 and Lemma A.7.** Use the fixed product parameters and rotation of Propositions \\(15\\)–\\(17\\).", "statement": "<p>**Biased product couplings — original Proposition A.8 and Lemma A.7.**</p><p>Use the fixed product parameters and rotation of Propositions \\(15\\)–\\(17\\).</p><p>For each \\(j\\), independently over \\(i\\in J_s\\), choose a labelled pair \\((x_i,y_i)\\) as follows. Put\n\\[\n\\rho_s(w)=\\kappa_s\\sqrt{w/m_s},\\qquad w=a_s,b_s.\n\\]\nFor each labelled width include top and bottom components of probability \\(\\rho_s(w)\\), with the same intervals as in Proposition \\(10\\). If \\(s=j\\), include a tag of probability \\(1/m_s\\), using\n\\[\nx_i\\in[0,1/m_s),\\qquad y_i\\in[1-1/m_s,1).\n\\]\nEach replacement component uses independent uniforms. With remaining probability\n\\[\nq_{j,s}=1-2\\rho_s(a_s)-2\\rho_s(b_s)-\\mathbf1_{\\{s=j\\}}/m_s,\n\\]\nset \\(x_i=y_i\\) uniformly. The remainder satisfies \\(q_{j,s}\\ge0.98\\).</p><p>Let \\((\\Omega_j,\\mathbb P_j)\\) retain the labels, and let \\(\\nu_j\\) be the endpoint law on \\(X^2\\). Then \\(x\\le y\\) almost surely.</p><p>Every coordinate endpoint density is at most \\(10\\). Both endpoint laws, and every deterministic coordinatewise hybrid marginal law, have \\(L^2(\\mu)\\)-density norm at most\n\\[\nH,\\qquad\nH^2=\\exp\\!\\left(20\\left(2\\sum_s\\kappa_s^2+1\\right)\\right).\n\\]\nConditional on \\(x\\), coordinates \\(y_i\\) are independent; conditional on \\(y\\), coordinates \\(x_i\\) are independent. If \\(i\\in J_s\\), \\(w=a_s\\) or \\(b_s\\), and \\(u\\in[w,1-w]\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(x_i&lt;u\\le y_i\\mid x)\\ge\\rho_s(w)/10\n\\quad\\text{on }\\{x_i&lt;u\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(x_i&lt;u\\le y_i\\mid y)\\ge\\rho_s(w)/10\n\\quad\\text{on }\\{y_i\\ge u\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Every invariant projection \\(p\\in M\\), \\((1-p)Tp=0\\), satisfies\n\\[\n(1-p(y))\\operatorname{diag}_kR_{x,y}(k)p(x)=0\n\\]\nfor \\(\\nu_j\\)-almost every pair, and all ratios are positive and at most \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(19.1\\) proves the marginal and crossing assertions. It remains to transfer the identity.</p><p>Let \\(y^{(D)}\\) use \\(y_i\\) for \\(i\\le D\\) and \\(x_i\\) thereafter, and let \\(\\beta_D\\) be the law of \\((x,y^{(D)})\\). Then\n\\[\n\\beta_D\\ll\\sum_{I\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,D\\}}\\lambda_I.\n\\]</p><p>To prove this, condition on a finite prefix pattern \\(c\\) of mixture labels, and let \\(I(c)\\) be its replacement coordinates. In each \\(i\\in I(c)\\), the conditional rectangle has density\n\\[\nr_{c,i}(t,z)=\n\\frac{\\mathbf1_{U_{c,i}}(t)\\mathbf1_{V_{c,i}}(z)}\n{|U_{c,i}||V_{c,i}|}\n\\]\nrelative to the unnormalized triangular law. The remaining prefix coordinates are uniformly diagonal.</p><p>The diagonal tail retains the biased \\(x\\)-law. If \\(g\\) is its full marginal density, define\n\\[\ng_D(z)=\\int g(u,z)\\,d\\mu_{\\le D}(u).\n\\]\nFubini identifies this as the density of \\(x_{&gt;D}\\), and Jensen gives\n\\[\n\\|g_D\\|_{L^2(\\mu_{&gt;D})}\\le H.\n\\]\nIndependence makes this tail law unchanged by the prefix conditioning. The conditional pair law therefore has density\n\\[\ng_D(x_{&gt;D})\\prod_{i\\in I(c)}r_{c,i}(x_i,y_i)\n\\]\nrelative to \\(\\lambda_{I(c)}\\). Summing over finitely many patterns proves the domination.</p><p>Proposition \\(18\\) gives the identity \\(\\beta_D\\)-almost everywhere. Its first marginal is the \\(x\\)-law, and its second uses \\(y\\)-marginals in the prefix and \\(x\\)-marginals in the tail. Lemma \\(19.1\\) therefore gives uniform absolute continuity for both marginal families.</p><p>Almost surely \\(y^{(D)}\\to y\\), so \\(\\beta_D\\Rightarrow\\nu_j\\). Every pair is ordered, all cocycle functions are finite at the endpoints almost surely, and every ratio is at most \\(1\\). Proposition \\(7\\) transfers the identity to \\(\\nu_j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 19.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Marginals and conditional crossings — original Lemma A.7.** The density bounds, hybrid bounds, conditional independence, and conditional crossing estimates in Proposition \\(19\\) hold.", "statement": "<p>**Marginals and conditional crossings — original Lemma A.7.**</p><p>The density bounds, hybrid bounds, conditional independence, and conditional crossing estimates in Proposition \\(19\\) hold. They may be required simultaneously for any specified countable family of thresholds.</p><p>For each endpoint or hybrid law \\(\\eta\\) and every Borel \\(A\\subset X\\),\n\\[\n\\eta(A)\\le H\\mu(A)^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The width bounds imply\n\\[\n\\rho_s(w)/w=\\kappa_s/\\sqrt{m_sw}\\le\\kappa_s.\n\\]\nEither endpoint density \\(h_i\\) satisfies\n\\[\n0&lt;q_{j,s}\\le h_i\n\\le1+4\\sum_{w=a_s,b_s}\\rho_s(w)/w+\\mathbf1_{\\{s=j\\}}\n\\le2+8\\kappa_s&lt;10.\n\\]</p><p>For a uniform interval density of interval length at least \\(w/2\\),\n\\[\n\\|v-1\\|_2^2\\le2/w.\n\\]\nFor a tagged interval, the bound is \\(m_s\\). Expressing \\(h_i-1\\) as at most five weighted mean-zero terms and applying Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\|h_i-1\\|_2^2\n\\le20\\sum_{w=a_s,b_s}\\frac{\\rho_s(w)^2}{w}\n+5\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\{s=j\\}}}{m_s}\n\\le20\\left(\\frac{2\\kappa_s^2}{m_s}\n+\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\{s=j\\}}}{m_s}\\right).\n\\]\nSum over blocks and apply Proposition \\(9\\). Cauchy–Schwarz gives the displayed set bound.</p><p>For a replacement label \\(\\ell\\), write its probability as \\(\\pi_\\ell\\), and its endpoint interval densities as \\(u_\\ell,v_\\ell\\). The first marginal density is\n\\[\nh_i^x(t)=q_{j,s}+\\sum_\\ell\\pi_\\ell u_\\ell(t).\n\\]\nA conditional law of \\(y_i\\) given \\(x_i=t\\) is\n\\[\nK_i^x(t,dz)\n=\\frac{q_{j,s}}{h_i^x(t)}\\delta_t(dz)\n+\\sum_\\ell\\frac{\\pi_\\ell u_\\ell(t)}{h_i^x(t)}v_\\ell(z)\\,dz.\n\\]\nThe reverse kernel interchanges \\(u_\\ell,v_\\ell\\). Independence of coordinate pairs shows on cylinder tests that the product kernels are conditional laws; the monotone class theorem extends this to Borel tests. This proves conditional independence.</p><p>If \\(x_i=t&lt;u\\le1-w\\), the top component's \\(x_i\\)-density is at least \\(1\\) at \\(t\\), and its whole \\(y_i\\)-interval lies above \\(u\\). Since \\(h_i^x\\le10\\), its conditional contribution is at least \\(\\rho_s(w)/10\\). The reverse estimate uses the bottom component when \\(y_i\\ge u\\ge w\\). Countable intersection gives simultaneous validity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The product convolution obstruction — Section A.5.** For the product operator \\(T=U\\pi(f)\\) of Proposition \\(17\\), every projection \\(p\\in M\\) satisfying \\( (1-p)Tp=0 \\) is \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).", "statement": "<p>**The product convolution obstruction — Section A.5.**</p><p>For the product operator \\(T=U\\pi(f)\\) of Proposition \\(17\\), every projection \\(p\\in M\\) satisfying\n\\[\n(1-p)Tp=0\n\\]\nis \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(p\\) is nontrivial. Use the notation of Lemma \\(20.1\\). Proposition \\(19\\) puts\n\\[\n\\zeta=\\operatorname{diag}_vR_{x,y}(v)\\xi(x)\n\\]\nin \\(\\operatorname{ran}p(y)\\). This range is invariant under every nonnegative power of \\(T(y)\\). Pairing \\(T(y)^n\\zeta\\) with \\(\\gamma(y)\\) yields\n\\[\n0=\n\\sum_{v\\in\\mathbb Z}\n\\frac{F_{n+v}(y)}{F_v(x)}\n\\xi_v(x)\\overline{\\gamma_{n+v}(y)}\n=\\sum_{v\\in\\mathbb Z}A_vB_{-n-v},\n\\qquad n\\ge0.\n\\]\nBy monotonicity,\n\\[\n0&lt;\n\\frac{F_{n+v}(y)}{F_v(x)}\n\\le\\min\\{R_{x,y}(v),R_{x,y}(n+v)\\}\\le1,\n\\]\nso the convolutions are absolutely convergent by Cauchy–Schwarz.</p><p>For sufficiently large fixed \\(j\\), apply Proposition \\(11\\) using Lemmas \\(20.1\\) and \\(20.2\\), with\n\\[\nK=K_j,\\quad D=D_j,\\quad a=1/j,\\quad c=c_*,\n\\quad\\eta=e^{-L_j},\\quad\\delta=e^{-L_j/400}.\n\\]\nIt gives\n\\[\nc_*\\varepsilon_j\n\\le(2K_j+1)(e^{-L_j}+2e^{-L_j/800})\n\\le(2K_j+1)3e^{-L_j/10000}\n\\le\\varepsilon_j/j.\n\\]\nCancel the positive \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) at this fixed stage. The resulting \\(c_*\\le1/j\\) is impossible for \\(j&gt;1/c_*\\).</p><p>The scales, angles, operator, and coupling laws were fixed before \\(p\\); only its conull representatives and the eventual event threshold depended on it.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Positive event and bounded finite coefficients.** Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(p\\) is a nontrivial invariant projection in the product model.", "statement": "<p>**Positive event and bounded finite coefficients.**</p><p>Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(p\\) is a nontrivial invariant projection in the product model. Put\n\\[\nr(x)=p(x)_{0,0},\\qquad\n\\xi(x)=p(x)e_0,\\qquad\n\\gamma(y)=(1-p(y))e_0,\n\\]\n\\[\nA_v=\\xi_v(x)/F_v(x),\\qquad\nB_v=F_{-v}(y)\\overline{\\gamma_{-v}(y)}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(K=K_j\\), \\(m=m_j\\), let \\(E_j\\) impose:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. for every \\(i\\in J_j\\),\n   \\[\n   x_i&lt;1-2/m,\\qquad y_i&gt;2/m,\n   \\]\n   with at least one tagged coordinate;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. for \\(s&gt;j\\), \\(i\\in J_s\\),\n   \\[\n   x_i&lt;1-2K/N_s,\\qquad y_i&gt;2K/N_s;\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(r(x)\\ge1/j\\) and \\(1-r(y)\\ge1/j\\).</p><p>For all sufficiently large \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(E_j)\\ge c_*,\\qquad c_*=e^{-82}/2,\n\\]\nand on \\(E_j\\),\n\\[\n|A_v|,|B_v|\\le D_j\\quad(|v|\\le K_j),\\qquad\n|A_0|,|B_0|\\ge1/j.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A tag at one current-block coordinate has probability \\(1/m\\) and satisfies its exclusions. By the coordinate density bound, a nontagged admissible choice has probability at least\n\\[\n1-\\frac1m-10\\frac2m-10\\frac2m=1-\\frac{41}{m}.\n\\]\nExactly one tag with admissible nontags elsewhere therefore has probability at least\n\\[\nm\\frac1m(1-41/m)^{m-1}\\ge e^{-82},\n\\]\nusing \\(m\\ge100\\) and \\(\\log(1-t)\\ge-2t\\) for \\(t\\le1/2\\).</p><p>Future exclusions lose at most\n\\[\n40K_j\\sum_{s&gt;j}K_s^{-1}\n\\le80K_j/K_{j+1}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nProposition \\(18\\) gives \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\) almost everywhere, and Proposition \\(19\\) bounds the remaining losses by\n\\[\nH\\mu\\{r&lt;1/j\\}^{1/2}\n+H\\mu\\{1-r&lt;1/j\\}^{1/2}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThis proves the event bound without requiring independence among its conditions.</p><p>For \\(0\\le v\\le K\\), the endpoint exclusions and angle bounds imply\n\\[\n\\lfloor x_i+v\\theta_i\\rfloor\n=\\lfloor y_i-v\\theta_i\\rfloor=0\n\\quad(i\\in J_s,\\ s\\ge j).\n\\]\nEarlier-coordinate floors have absolute value at most \\(K+1\\). Hence\n\\[\n1/F_v(x)\\le D_j,\\qquad F_{-v}(y)\\le D_j.\n\\]\nFor negative coefficient indices, monotonicity gives\n\\[\nF_{-v}(x)\\ge1,\\qquad F_v(y)\\le1\\quad(v\\ge0),\n\\]\nso their coefficients have modulus at most \\(1\\). Finally,\n\\[\nA_0=r(x),\\qquad B_0=1-r(y).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Product attenuation at every omitted index.** Under the hypothetical projection and events of Lemma \\(20.1\\), for every sufficiently large \\(j\\) and every \\(|k|>K_j\\), there are events \\(H_k^{(1)},H_k^{(2)}\\) such …", "statement": "<p>**Product attenuation at every omitted index.**</p><p>Under the hypothetical projection and events of Lemma \\(20.1\\), for every sufficiently large \\(j\\) and every \\(|k|&gt;K_j\\), there are events \\(H_k^{(1)},H_k^{(2)}\\) such that\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(k)\n\\le e^{-L_j}+\\mathbf1_{H_k^{(1)}}+\\mathbf1_{H_k^{(2)}}\n\\quad\\text{on }E_j,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(H_k^{(1)}\\mid x)\\le e^{-L_j/400},\\qquad\n\\mathbb P_j(H_k^{(2)}\\mid y)\\le e^{-L_j/400}\n\\]\nunder the full law.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(K_j&lt;|k|\\le N_j/4\\), use a tag in \\(J_j\\):\n\\[\n1/m_j&lt;|k|\\theta_i\\le1/2,\\qquad\nx_i&lt;1/m_j,\\quad y_i\\ge1-1/m_j.\n\\]\nThe threshold is crossed for either sign of \\(k\\), giving\n\\[\nR_{x,y}(k)\\le e^{-C_j}\\le e^{-L_j}\n\\]\non \\(E_j\\). Both exceptional events are empty.</p><p>Choose \\(j\\) so large that Proposition \\(15\\)'s phase assertions hold in every block \\(s\\ge j\\). Put\n\\[\n\\alpha_s=G_sK_s,\\qquad \\beta_s=N_s/4,\\qquad\nQ_s=m_s^{(4/5)m_s}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\alpha_s\\le\\beta_s&lt;Q_s,\\qquad\n\\alpha_{s+1}&lt;Q_s,\\qquad Q_s\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThus \\([\\beta_j,Q_j]\\), together with \\([\\alpha_s,Q_s]\\) for \\(s&gt;j\\), covers \\([\\beta_j,\\infty)\\).</p><p>For each remaining \\(k\\), choose deterministically a qualifying block and width, using \\(w=b_s\\) with \\(s\\ge j\\), or \\(w=a_s\\) with \\(s&gt;j\\). There is a deterministic list \\(I(k)\\subset J_s\\), of size \\(q\\ge m_s/20\\), with\n\\[\nu_i=1-\\{k\\theta_i\\}\\in[w,1-w].\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nm_s\\rho_s(b_s)=\\kappa_sm_s^{1/40}\\ge L_s\\ge L_j,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nm_s\\rho_s(a_s)=\\kappa_s\\sqrt{G_s}\\ge L_{s-1}\\ge L_j,\n\\]\nand \\(C_s\\ge L_j\\).</p><p>Let \\(B_k\\) be the event that no listed threshold is crossed. Set\n\\[\na_k(x)=\\#\\{i\\in I(k):x_i&lt;u_i\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nH_k^{(1)}=B_k\\cap\\{a_k(x)\\ge q/2\\},\\qquad\nH_k^{(2)}=B_k\\cap\\{a_k(x)&lt;q/2\\}.\n\\]\nGiven \\(x\\), conditional independence and Proposition \\(19\\) imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_j(H_k^{(1)}\\mid x)\n\\le(1-\\rho_s(w)/10)^{q/2}\n\\le e^{-m_s\\rho_s(w)/400}\n\\le e^{-L_j/400}.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(B_k\\), order and no crossing imply\n\\[\nx_i&lt;u_i\\iff y_i&lt;u_i.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nH_k^{(2)}\n=B_k\\cap\\{\\#\\{i\\in I(k):y_i\\ge u_i\\}&gt;q/2\\}\n\\]\nalmost surely. Reverse conditional independence gives the other bound.</p><p>Outside \\(B_k\\), one coordinate crosses, giving \\(R_{x,y}(k)\\le e^{-C_s}\\le e^{-L_j}\\); on \\(B_k\\), the ratio is at most \\(1\\). Countability permits simultaneous conditional versions. No union bound over all omitted indices is taken.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite atomic algebras and a diffuse full orbit — original Proposition A.9.** For the product operator \\(T\\) of Proposition \\(17\\), its polar decomposition is \\( T=U|T|,\\qquad |T|=\\pi(f),\\qquad \\ker|T|=\\{0\\}. \\) The …", "statement": "<p>**Finite atomic algebras and a diffuse full orbit — original Proposition A.9.**</p><p>For the product operator \\(T\\) of Proposition \\(17\\), its polar decomposition is\n\\[\nT=U|T|,\\qquad |T|=\\pi(f),\\qquad \\ker|T|=\\{0\\}.\n\\]\nThe spectral measure of \\(|T|\\) is countably atomic.</p><p>For every finite \\(F\\subset\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\nA_F=W^*(U^n|T|U^{-n}:n\\in F)\n\\]\nis atomic. The full abelian algebra\n\\[\nA=W^*(U^n|T|U^{-n}:n\\in\\mathbb Z)\n\\]\nis diffuse. This does not identify \\(A\\) with all of \\(\\pi(L^\\infty(X))\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(f&gt;0\\) almost everywhere,\n\\[\nT^*T=\\pi(f)^2,\\qquad |T|=\\pi(f),\n\\]\nand \\(|T|\\) has zero kernel. Thus \\(U\\) is its polar unitary.</p><p>Almost everywhere, the sum defining \\(f\\) has finite support. Its value is determined by a finite subset of a countable coordinate set. There are only countably many such subsets, so \\(f\\) has countable essential range in \\((0,1]\\). Its nonnull level sets give countably many spectral projections summing to \\(1\\).</p><p>The representation convention gives\n\\[\nU\\pi(g)U^*=\\pi(g\\circ\\sigma^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor finite \\(F\\), the tuple \\((f\\circ\\sigma^{-n})_{n\\in F}\\) has countable range on a common conull set. Its nonnull joint level sets give an atomic partition of \\(A_F\\). This includes \\(F=\\varnothing\\), when the generated unital algebra is scalar.</p><p>The full algebra \\(A\\) is an invariant abelian subalgebra of \\(\\pi(L^\\infty(X))\\). The rotation is totally ergodic: for every nonzero integer \\(q\\) and nonconstant finite-support character \\(v\\), rational independence gives\n\\[\nq\\sum_i v_i\\theta_i\\notin\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nThe Fourier argument proves ergodicity of \\(\\sigma^q\\).</p><p>If \\(e\\) were an atom of \\(A\\), its integer translates under \\(\\operatorname{Ad}U\\) would be atoms of equal positive trace. Distinct atoms in an abelian algebra are orthogonal, so this orbit is finite. For some \\(q&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nU^qeU^{-q}=e.\n\\]\nSince \\(e\\in\\pi(L^\\infty(X))\\), total ergodicity forces \\(e=1\\). Then \\(A\\) would be scalar.</p><p>But \\(A\\) contains the nonconstant \\(\\pi(f)\\): a positive almost-everywhere constant would have finite logarithmic integral, contrary to Proposition \\(17\\). Hence \\(A\\) has no atoms.</p>"}], "edges": [[5, 4, "part"], [6, 4, "part"], [12, 23, "cite"], [17, 16, "part"], [18, 16, "part"], [21, 20, "part"], [22, 20, "part"], [25, 24, "part"], [26, 24, "part"], [30, 29, "part"], [31, 29, "part"], [34, 33, "part"], [36, 35, "part"], [37, 35, "part"]]});
